{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22276","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22276","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22276","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Issa","familyName":"Sugiura","name":"Sugiura, Issa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhei","familyName":"Kurita","name":"Kurita, Shuhei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Oda","name":"Oda, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daisuke","familyName":"Kawahara","name":"Kawahara, Daisuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yasuo","familyName":"Okabe","name":"Okabe, Yasuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naoaki","familyName":"Okazaki","name":"Okazaki, Naoaki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"WAON: A Large-Scale Japanese Image-Text Dataset for Cultural Adaptation in Contrastive Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T12:42:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:35:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-01T12:35:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-02T00:49:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-31T10:16:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-02T01:07:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-02T02:27:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-10-05T00:25:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Contrastive vision-language models have achieved remarkable progress through large-scale pretraining. Recent work has shown that removing English-only caption filters and pretraining on global data is effective for improving multicultural performance. We study whether such global pretraining is sufficient for culture-specific understanding, or whether further adaptation with natively sourced data can boost performance beyond what global pretraining alone achieves. To enable this investigation, we present WAON, the largest publicly available native Japanese image-text dataset constructed from native Japanese web content in Common Crawl, containing approximately 155 million examples. We also introduce WAON-Bench, a manually curated Japanese cultural benchmark spanning 374 classes. Through comparative fine-tuning experiments on multiple Japanese image-text datasets, we observe that models fine-tuned on WAON consistently achieve stronger performance on Japanese cultural benchmarks than those fine-tuned on English-to-Japanese translated data. Controlled experiments at matched scale, filtering, and training budget across two model families further indicate that native web origin is the primary driver of this gain. We release our dataset, benchmark, model, and code."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to AACL 2026 (Findings)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22276","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:31Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-05T02:43:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hetian","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Hetian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CityRiSE: Reasoning Urban Socio-Economic Status in Large Vision-Language Models via Reinforcement Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T12:56:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:35:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T11:41:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:44:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Urban socio-economic sensing plays a vital role in advancing global sustainable development goals. With the advent of Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), new opportunities have emerged to address this challenge by framing it as a multi-modal perception and reasoning task. However, recent studies show that LVLMs still struggle to make accurate and interpretable socio-economic predictions from visual data. To overcome these limitations and fully exploit the potential of LVLMs, we propose CityRiSE, a novel framework for Reasoning urban Socio-Economic status in LVLMs via reinforcement learning (RL). With carefully curated multi-modal dataset and verifiable reward design, our approach guides the LVLM to focus on semantically meaningful visual cues, enabling structured and goal-oriented reasoning for generalist socio-economic status prediction. Experiments demonstrate that CityRiSE, equipped with emergent reasoning, significantly outperforms existing baselines, improving both prediction accuracy and generalization across diverse urban contexts, especially on unseen cities and unseen indicators. This work highlights the promise of combining RL and LVLMs for interpretable and generalist urban socio-economic sensing."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ACM MM 2026, https://github.com/tsinghua-fib-lab/CityRiSE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:11:39Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:11:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:23:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21805","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21805","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21805","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Yichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiqing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yiqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoping","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Guoping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruiming","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ruiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Gai","name":"Gai, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guorui","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Guorui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DiffGRM: Diffusion-based Generative Recommendation Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-21T03:23:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-13T09:23:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-14T00:37:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3774904.3792156","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative recommendation (GR) is an emerging paradigm that represents each item via a tokenizer as an n-digit semantic ID (SID) and predicts the next item by autoregressively generating its SID conditioned on the user's history. However, two structural properties of SIDs make ARMs ill-suited. First, intra-item consistency: the n digits jointly specify one item, yet the left-to-right causality trains each digit only under its prefix and blocks bidirectional cross-digit evidence, collapsing supervision to a single causal path. Second, inter-digit heterogeneity: digits differ in semantic granularity and predictability, while the uniform next-token objective assigns equal weight to all digits, overtraining easy digits and undertraining hard digits. To address these two issues, we propose DiffGRM, a diffusion-based GR model that replaces the autoregressive decoder with a masked discrete diffusion model (MDM), thereby enabling bidirectional context and any-order parallel generation of SID digits for recommendation. Specifically, we tailor DiffGRM in three aspects: (1) tokenization with Parallel Semantic Encoding (PSE) to decouple digits and balance per-digit information; (2) training with On-policy Coherent Noising (OCN) that prioritizes uncertain digits via coherent masking to concentrate supervision on high-value signals; and (3) inference with Confidence-guided Parallel Denoising (CPD) that fills higher-confidence digits first and generates diverse Top-K candidates. Experiments show consistent gains over strong generative and discriminative recommendation baselines on multiple datasets, improving NDCG@10 by 6.9%-15.5%. Code is available at https://github.com/liuzhao09/DiffGRM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures. Accepted at The ACM Web Conference 2026. Camera-ready version; author list updated to match the published version; presentation revised, results unchanged"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21805","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:22Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-14T02:23:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22665","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22665","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22665","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiwei","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Qiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xukun","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Xukun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Puhong","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Puhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xudong","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Xudong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shutao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Shutao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SARVLM: A Vision Language Foundation Model for Semantic Understanding in SAR Imagery"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T13:04:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:00:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-26T10:05:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-27T01:38:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T05:24:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-05-18T00:27:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-08-08T09:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-08-11T00:27:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Synthetic Aperture Radar (SAR) is a critical imaging modality due to its all-weather operational capability. Although recent advances in self-supervised learning and masked image modeling (MIM) have enabled SAR foundation models, these approaches primarily focus on low-level visual features and often neglect multi-modal representation. Moreover, multimodal data for SAR is scarce, limiting the development of robust cross-modal models. To address this limitation, we construct SARVLM-1M, a large-scale vision-language dataset comprising over one million image-text pairs aggregated from existing datasets. Furthermore, to mitigate the substantial differences between SAR and natural imagery, we propose a two-stage domain transfer training strategy that leverages optical remote sensing data as an intermediate bridge, facilitating effective knowledge transfer from natural images to SAR domains. Based on this strategy, we develop SARVLM, the first vision-language foundation model tailored for SAR, consisting of SARCLIP and SARCap. In addition, an ensemble strategy is utilized to improve the cross-scene generalization capability of the model. Moreover, SARDet and SARRot further validate the capability of the proposed framework in object detection. Extensive experiments on 13 benchmarks across image-text retrieval, target recognition, zero-shot classification, object detection, semantic localization, and image captioning demonstrate the superior feature extraction and interpretation capabilities of SARVLM. It consistently outperforms state-of-the-art vision-language models and advances semantic understanding in SAR imagery. Code and datasets will be released on https://github.com/KlayMa527/SARVLM.git."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 13 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22665","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-11T04:13:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22868","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22868","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22868","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qianyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qianyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Imani","name":"Imani, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiong","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing the Unseen: Towards Training-Free Inspection for Wind Turbine Blades Using Knowledge-Augmented Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T23:19:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:12:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-08-01T15:11:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-08-04T00:37:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Wind turbine blades operate in harsh environments, making timely damage detection essential for preventing failures and optimizing maintenance. Drone-based inspection and deep learning are promising, but typically depend on large, labeled datasets, which limit their ability to detect rare or evolving damage types. To address this, we propose a zero-shot-oriented inspection framework that integrates Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with Vision-Language Models (VLM). A multimodal knowledge base is constructed, comprising technical documentation, representative reference images, and domain-specific guidelines. A hybrid text-image retriever with keyword-aware reranking assembles the most relevant context to condition the VLM at inference, injecting domain knowledge without task-specific training. We evaluate the framework on 30 labeled blade images covering diverse damage categories. Although the dataset is small due to the difficulty of acquiring verified blade imagery, it covers multiple representative defect types. On this test set, the RAG-grounded VLM correctly classified all samples, whereas the same VLM without retrieval performed worse in both accuracy and precision. We further compare against open-vocabulary baselines and incorporate uncertainty Clopper-Pearson confidence intervals to account for the small-sample setting. Ablation studies indicate that the key advantage of the framework lies in explainability and generalizability: retrieved references ground the reasoning process and enable the detection of previously unseen defects by leveraging domain knowledge rather than relying solely on visual cues. This research contributes a data-efficient solution for industrial inspection that reduces dependence on extensive labeled datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22868","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-08-04T04:36:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23509","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23509","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23509","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weizheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weizheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Obi","familyName":"Ike","name":"Ike, Obi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soyun","familyName":"Choi","name":"Choi, Soyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungeun","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Sungeun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniket","familyName":"Bera","name":"Bera, Aniket","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Byung-Cheol","familyName":"Min","name":"Min, Byung-Cheol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Logic-Guided Socially-aware Robot Navigation World Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:47:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-24T15:58:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-26T01:09:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-07-18T06:00:54Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; The authors have decided to withdraw this manuscript due to concerns regarding its current scope, framing, and presentation. Please do not cite this version"},{"date":"2026-07-21T00:22:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Social robot navigation increasingly relies on large language models for reasoning, path planning, and enabling movement in dynamic human spaces. However, relying solely on LLMs for planning often leads to unpredictable and unsafe behaviors, especially in dynamic human spaces, due to limited physical grounding and weak logical consistency. In this work, we introduce NaviWM, a socially-aware robot Navigation World Model that augments LLM reasoning with a structured world model and a logic-driven chain-of-thought process. NaviWM consists of two main components: (1) a spatial-temporal world model that captures the positions, velocities, and activities of agents in the environment, and (2) a deductive reasoning module that guides LLMs through a multi-step, logic-based inference process. This integration enables the robot to generate navigation decisions that are both socially compliant and physically safe, under well-defined constraints such as personal space, collision avoidance, and timing. Unlike previous methods based on prompting or fine-tuning, NaviWM encodes social norms as first-order logic, enabling interpretable and verifiable reasoning. Experiments show that NaviWM improves success rates and reduces social violations, particularly in crowded environments. These results demonstrate the benefit of combining formal reasoning with LLMs for robust social navigation. Additional experimental details and demo videos for this work can be found at: https://sites.google.com/view/NaviWM."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The authors have decided to withdraw this manuscript due to concerns regarding its current scope, framing, and presentation. Please do not cite this version"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23509","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-07-21T03:43:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Walid","familyName":"Bousselham","name":"Bousselham, Walid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hilde","familyName":"Kuehne","name":"Kuehne, Hilde","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cordelia","familyName":"Schmid","name":"Schmid, Cordelia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VOLD: Reasoning Transfer from LLMs to Vision-Language Models via On-Policy Distillation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:32:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T11:09:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:45:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-03T18:11:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:05:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training vision-language models (VLMs) for complex reasoning remains a challenging task, i.a. due to the scarcity of high-quality image-text reasoning data. Conversely, text-based reasoning resources are abundant and scalable, but it is still an open question how to leveraging them for VLM reasoning. To address this problem, we propose VOLD, a framework to transfer reasoning capabilities from text-only teacher models to VLM student models. To this end, VOLD combines reinforcement learning via Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with on-policy distillation, which allows the student reasoning traces to be guided by the teacher model, resulting in a significant gain over using GRPO alone. We further show that a cold-start alignment is essential for an effective transfer during the online training phase in this scenario and that without sufficient distributional alignment between teacher and student, on-policy distillation fails to provide meaningful guidance. We evaluate VOLD across diverse benchmarks including MMMU-Pro, MathVision, MathVista, and LogicVista, showing that VOLD outperforms the baseline model significantly and improves over the state of the art by a margin. Our ablation shows the importance of a cold-start alignment via SFT for on-policy distillation with a text-only teacher."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"www.walidbousselham.com/VOLD/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22768","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22768","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22768","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yilun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav Narayanan","familyName":"Venkit","name":"Venkit, Pranav Narayanan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kung-Hsiang","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Kung-Hsiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiaxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nanyun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Nanyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chien-Sheng","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Chien-Sheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Seeing is Believing? Evaluating Vision-Language Model Susceptibility in Agent-to-Agent Multimodal Persuasion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T17:39:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-02T23:23:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-05T00:01:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As autonomous agents increasingly interact, they inevitably attempt to influence one another. While prior work in text-only settings has explored the dynamics of Agent-to-Agent (A2A) persuasion, the rise of Vision-Language Models (VLMs) introduces a more complex challenge: multimodal content conveys richer information while integrating subtle, hard-to-detect persuasive cues. To study this vulnerability, we present MMPersuade, a unified framework and dataset for A2A multimodal persuasion. We model interactions between a persuader agent, which leverages images and psychological strategies, and a persuadee VLM. Our benchmark spans commercial, subjective and behavioral, and adversarial contexts, and evaluates persuasion via function-calling that capture behavioral shifts beyond verbal responses. Experiments on six VLMs reveal three findings: (1) multimodal inputs consistently outperform text-only persuasion, with raw visual signals uniquely increasing susceptibility in adversarial settings by bypassing text-activated safety defenses; (2) persuadee vulnerability is highly domain- and format-dependent, with realistic and community-style formats driving susceptibility in commercial settings while different formats dominate in adversarial ones; and (3) psychological strategy efficacy varies with context and model architecture, as more capable models resist benign persuasion yet become more susceptible under adversarial multimodal inputs. Our framework provides a foundation for building more robust and aligned VLMs in multi-agent environments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22768","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:08Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-05T07:04:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tengchao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tengchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sichen","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Sichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengzhao","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Mengzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaming","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jiaming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanyang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yuanyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MMTutorBench: The First Multimodal Benchmark for AI Math Tutoring"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:11:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:52:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-13T19:10:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-15T00:07:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Effective math tutoring requires not only solving problems but also diagnosing students' difficulties and guiding them step by step. While multimodal large language models (MLLMs) show promise, existing benchmarks largely overlook these tutoring skills. We introduce MMTutorBench, the first benchmark for AI math tutoring, consisting of 685 problems built around pedagogically significant key-steps. Each problem is paired with problem-specific rubrics that enable fine-grained evaluation across six dimensions, and structured into three tasks-Insight Discovery, Operation Formulation, and Operation Execution. We evaluate 12 leading MLLMs and find clear performance gaps between proprietary and open-source systems, substantial room compared to human tutors, and consistent trends across input variants: OCR pipelines degrade tutoring quality, few-shot prompting yields limited gains, and our rubric-based LLM-as-a-Judge proves highly reliable. These results highlight both the difficulty and diagnostic value of MMTutorBench for advancing AI tutoring."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:01Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-15T02:36:18Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21828","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21828","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21828","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingbing","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Lingbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huajun","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Huajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structured and Abstractive Reasoning on Multi-modal Relational Knowledge Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T02:23:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-29T06:52:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-30T00:28:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding and reasoning with abstractive information from the visual modality presents significant challenges for current multi-modal large language models (MLLMs). Among the various forms of abstractive information, Multi-Modal Relational Knowledge (MMRK), which represents abstract relational structures between multi-modal entities using node-edge formats, remains largely under-explored. In particular, STructured and Abstractive Reasoning (STAR) on such data has received little attention from the research community. To bridge the dual gaps in large-scale high-quality data and capability enhancement methodologies, this paper makes the following key contributions: (i). An automatic STAR data engine capable of synthesizing images with MMRK to build multi-modal instruction data with reliable chain-of-thought thinking for various STAR tasks and (ii). A comprehsive two-stage capability enhancement training framework, accompanied by a suite of evaluation protocols tailored to different STAR tasks. Based upon these contributions, we introduce STAR-64K, a dataset comprising 64K high-quality multi-modal instruction samples, and conduct experiments across 5 open-source MLLMs. Experimental results show that our two-stage enhancement framework enables smaller 3B/7B models to significantly outperform GPT-4o in STAR. Additionally, we provide in-depth analysis regarding the effectiveness of various designs, data transferability, and scalability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by Findings of ACL 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21828","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-30T02:20:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23095","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23095","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23095","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuejing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xuejing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sibo","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Sibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruibing","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Ruibing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hong","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Hong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junyang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Junyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Multimodal Positional Encoding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T08:00:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:28:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-05T14:25:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T01:46:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T07:18:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-07T01:16:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal position encoding is essential for vision-language models, yet there has been little systematic investigation into multimodal position encoding. We conduct a comprehensive analysis of multimodal Rotary Positional Embedding (RoPE) by examining its two core components: position design and frequency allocation. Through extensive experiments, we identify three key guidelines: positional coherence, full frequency utilization, and preservation of textual priors-ensuring unambiguous layout, rich representation, and faithful transfer from the pre-trained LLM. Based on these insights, we propose Multi-Head RoPE (MHRoPE) and MRoPE-Interleave (MRoPE-I), two simple and plug-and-play variants that require no architectural changes. Our methods consistently outperform existing approaches across diverse benchmarks, with significant improvements in both general and fine-grained multimodal understanding. Code will be avaliable at https://github.com/JJJYmmm/Multimodal-RoPEs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"16 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23095","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-07T03:27:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22201","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22201","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22201","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minho","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Minho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kinam","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Kinam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junha","familyName":"Hyung","name":"Hyung, Junha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyojin","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Hyojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hoiyeong","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Hoiyeong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jooyeol","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Jooyeol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojoon","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hojoon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jaegul","familyName":"Choo","name":"Choo, Jaegul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ACG: Action Coherence Guidance for Flow-based Vision-Language-Action models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T07:44:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:29:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-25T14:00:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-26T00:56:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion and flow matching models have emerged as powerful robot policies, enabling Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models to generalize across diverse scenes and instructions. Yet, when trained via imitation learning, their high generative capacity makes them sensitive to noise in human demonstrations: jerks, pauses, and jitter which reduce action coherence. Reduced action coherence causes instability and trajectory drift during deployment, failures that are catastrophic in fine-grained manipulation where precision is crucial. In this paper, we present Action Coherence Guidance (ACG) for VLA models, a training-free test-time guidance algorithm that improves action coherence and thereby yields performance gains. Evaluated on RoboCasa, DexMimicGen, and real-world SO-101 tasks, ACG consistently improves action coherence and boosts success rates across diverse manipulation tasks. Code and project page are available at https://github.com/DAVIAN-Robotics/ACG and https://DAVIAN-Robotics.github.io/ACG , respectively."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ICRA 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22201","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:09:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:09:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-26T02:30:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing-Xuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jing-Xuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Genshun","familyName":"Wan","name":"Wan, Genshun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianqing","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Jianqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Duo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhen-Hua","familyName":"Ling","name":"Ling, Zhen-Hua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting Speech Foundation Models for Unified Multimodal Speech Recognition with Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:36:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-27T23:53:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-03T01:17:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While speech foundation models (SFMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance in audio-only tasks, their adaptation to multimodal scenarios remains underexplored. This work presents UASR-LLM, a novel framework that adapts frozen SFMs to unified visual speech recognition (VSR), automatic speech recognition (ASR), and audio-visual speech recognition (AVSR) by leveraging large language models (LLMs) as text decoders. Visual representations are injected into multiple SFM layers via visual injection modules, enabling multimodal fusion and unified representation learning. The augmented SFMs are connected to decoder-only LLMs through a feed-forward adaptor, where concatenated representations and instruction prompts guide transcription. We propose a two-stage training strategy consisting of visual injection pretraining followed by speech recognition finetuning. The pretraining stage aligns audio, visual, and audio-visual representations within the frozen SFM backbone, while the finetuning stage integrates LLMs for unified optimization across speech recognition tasks. Experimental results demonstrate superior performance over state-of-the-art baselines across VSR, ASR, and AVSR under both clean and noisy conditions. Ablation studies further confirm generalization across various SFMs and LLMs, validating the effectiveness of the proposed training strategy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-03T05:47:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23479","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23479","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23479","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Siyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siyong","familyName":"Jian","name":"Jian, Siyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Huan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MergeMix: A Unified Augmentation Paradigm for Visual and Multi-Modal Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:12:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:52:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-29T08:02:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-30T01:57:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-23T17:06:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-24T02:40:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language alignment in multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) relies on supervised fine-tuning (SFT) or reinforcement learning (RL). To align multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) in the post-training stage, supervised fine-tuning (SFT) is a stable choice but requires human annotations and lacks task generalizations, while Reinforcement Learning (RL) searches for better answers from reward signals but suffers from computational overhead and instability. To achieve balance among scalability, efficiency, and alignment generalizations, we propose MergeMix, a unified paradigm that bridges SFT and RL with an efficient Token Merge based Mixup augmentation. As for the Mixup policy, we generate contextual aligned mixed images with the corresponding labels according to the merged attention maps with cluster regions. Then, we enhance the preference-driven paradigm for MLLMs by building preference pairs with raw images and MergeMix-generated ones and optimizing the soft preference margin with the mixed SimPO loss. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MergeMix not only achieves dominant classification accuracy as an augmentation method but also improves generalization abilities and alignment of MLLMs, providing a new learning paradigm for preference alignment with training efficiency and stability."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026, Web link: https://jinxins.github.io/MergeMix_Web/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23479","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-24T04:01:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22936","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22936","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22936","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mouxiao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Mouxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Borui","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Borui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dehua","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Dehua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hailin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Hailin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PPE: Positional Preservation Embedding for Token Compression in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T02:40:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:17:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-05T08:25:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-06T01:32:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have achieved strong performance on vision-language tasks, yet often suffer from inefficiencies due to redundant visual tokens. Existing token merging methods reduce sequence length but frequently disrupt spatial layouts and temporal continuity by disregarding positional relationships. In this work, we propose a novel encoding operator dubbed as \\textbf{P}ositional \\textbf{P}reservation \\textbf{E}mbedding (\\textbf{PPE}), which has the main hallmark of preservation of spatiotemporal structure during visual token compression. PPE explicitly introduces the disentangled encoding of 3D positions in the token dimension, enabling each compressed token to encapsulate different positions from multiple original tokens. Furthermore, we show that PPE can effectively support cascade clustering -- a progressive token compression strategy that leads to better performance retention. PPE is a parameter-free and generic operator that can be seamlessly integrated into existing token merging methods without any adjustments. Applied to state-of-the-art token merging framework, PPE achieves consistent improvements of $2\\%\\sim5\\%$ across multiple vision-language benchmarks, including MMBench (general vision understanding), TextVQA (layout understanding) and VideoMME (temporal understanding). These results demonstrate that preserving positional cues is critical for efficient and effective MLLM reasoning. Our code is available at https://github.com/MouxiaoHuang/PPE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22936","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:05Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-06T03:22:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21850","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21850","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21850","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyubeum","familyName":"Lim","name":"Lim, Gyubeum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yemo","familyName":"Koo","name":"Koo, Yemo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay Krishna","familyName":"Madisetti","name":"Madisetti, Vijay Krishna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SCoPE VLM: Selective Context Processing for Efficient Document Navigation in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T17:47:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T16:39:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T02:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Understanding long-context visual information remains a fundamental challenge for vision-language models, particularly in agentic tasks such as GUI control and web navigation. While web pages and GUI environments are inherently structured documents, current VLMs typically neglect decision-oriented document understanding in their training objectives. Existing approaches primarily extend visual embeddings to process long, high-resolution inputs, but these methods are memory-intensive and impractical for locally deployable solutions. To address these issues, we propose SCoPE VLM, a document navigation expert that leverages a novel Chain of Scroll mechanism to selectively and recursively navigate documents, focusing exclusively on relevant segments. We introduce a dedicated data generation pipeline to construct informative Chain of Scroll trajectories and Episodic Group Relative Policy Optimization, a tailored reinforcement learning method to bridge the gap between training and inference. Our method substantially reduces memory usage and effectively models human-like reading behaviors. To the best of our knowledge, SCoPE VLM is the first framework to explicitly model agentic reading patterns in multi-page document question answering, advancing the capabilities of multimodal agents."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21850","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-28T03:24:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Muhammad Tayyab","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Muhammad Tayyab","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zane","familyName":"Yong","name":"Yong, Zane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lequn","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lequn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenhe","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Wenhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas Yew Jin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Nicholas Yew Jin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung Ki","familyName":"Moon","name":"Moon, Seung Ki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multi-Stage Hybrid Framework for Automated Interpretation of Multi-View Engineering Drawings Using Vision Language Model"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T09:07:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-23T11:39:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-26T01:32:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Engineering drawings are fundamental to manufacturing communication, serving as the primary medium for conveying design intent, tolerances, and production details. However, interpreting complex multi-view drawings with dense annotations remains challenging using manual methods, generic optical character recognition (OCR) systems, or traditional deep learning approaches, due to varied layouts, orientations, and mixed symbolic-textual content. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a three-stage hybrid framework for the automated interpretation of 2D multi-view engineering drawings using modern detection and vision language models (VLMs). In the first stage, YOLOv11-det performs layout segmentation to localize key regions such as views, title blocks, and notes. The second stage uses YOLOv11-obb for orientation-aware, fine-grained detection of annotations, including measures, GD\u0026amp;T symbols, and surface roughness indicators. The third stage employs two Donut-based, OCR-free VLMs for semantic content parsing: the Alphabetical VLM extracts textual and categorical information from title blocks and notes, while the Numerical VLM interprets quantitative data such as measures, GD\u0026amp;T frames, and surface roughness. Two specialized datasets were developed to ensure robustness and generalization: 1,000 drawings for layout detection and 1,406 for annotation-level training. The Alphabetical VLM achieved an overall F1 score of 0.672, while the Numerical VLM reached 0.963, demonstrating strong performance in textual and quantitative interpretation, respectively. The unified JSON output enables seamless integration with CAD and manufacturing databases, providing a scalable solution for intelligent engineering drawing analysis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This draft has been accepted in the 13th International Conference on Industrial Engineering and Applications (ICIEA 2026)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:42Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-26T02:50:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21986","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21986","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21986","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dogyun","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Dogyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moayed","familyName":"Haji-Ali","name":"Haji-Ali, Moayed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Willi","familyName":"Menapace","name":"Menapace, Willi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sergey","familyName":"Tulyakov","name":"Tulyakov, Sergey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwoo J.","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyunwoo J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aliaksandr","familyName":"Siarohin","name":"Siarohin, Aliaksandr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anil","familyName":"Kag","name":"Kag, Anil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sprint: Sparse-Dense Residual Fusion for Efficient Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T19:29:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:10:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:58:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-12T01:11:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers (DiTs) deliver state-of-the-art generative performance but their quadratic training cost with sequence length makes large-scale pretraining prohibitively expensive. Token dropping can reduce training cost, yet naïve strategies degrade representations, and existing methods are either parameter-heavy or fail at high drop ratios. We present SPRINT, Sparse--Dense Residual Fusion for Efficient Diffusion Transformers, a simple method that enables aggressive token dropping (up to 75%) while preserving quality. SPRINT leverages the complementary roles of shallow and deep layers: early layers process all tokens to capture local detail, deeper layers operate on a sparse subset to cut computation, and their outputs are fused through residual connections. Training follows a two-stage schedule: long masked pre-training for efficiency followed by short full-token fine-tuning to close the train--inference gap. On ImageNet-1K 256x256, SPRINT achieves 9.8x training savings with comparable FID/FDD, and at inference, its Path-Drop Guidance (PDG) nearly halves FLOPs while improving quality. These results establish SPRINT as a simple, effective, and general solution for efficient DiT training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21986","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:04:36Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:04:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-12T02:42:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyeongseop","familyName":"Rha","name":"Rha, Hyeongseop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jeong Hun","familyName":"Yeo","name":"Yeo, Jeong Hun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yeonju","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Yeonju","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong Man","familyName":"Ro","name":"Ro, Yong Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Emotion-Coherent Reasoning for Multimodal LLMs via Emotional Rationale Verifier"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:40:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-12T04:50:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-13T01:23:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T15:47:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:59:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-05T13:24:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-06T02:15:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The recent advancement of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) is transforming human-computer interaction (HCI) from surface-level exchanges into more nuanced and emotionally intelligent communication. To realize this shift, emotion understanding becomes essential allowing systems to capture subtle cues underlying user intent. Furthermore, providing faithful explanations for predicted emotions is crucial to ensure interpretability and build user trust. However, current MLLM-based methods often generate emotion explanations that diverge from the target labels and sometimes even contradict their own predicted emotions. This inconsistency poses a critical risk for misunderstanding and erodes reliability in interactive settings. To address this, we propose a novel approach: the Emotional Rationale Verifier (ERV) and an Explanation Reward. Our method guides the model to produce reasoning that is explicitly consistent with the target emotion during multimodal emotion recognition without modifying the model architecture or requiring additional paired video-description annotations. Our method significantly improves faithful explanation-prediction consistency and explanation emotion accuracy on the MAFW and DFEW datasets. Through extensive experiments and human evaluations, we show that our approach not only enhances alignment between explanation and prediction but also empowers MLLMs to deliver emotionally coherent, trustworthy interactions, marking a key step toward truly human-like HCI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 11 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:43Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:11:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22535","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22535","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22535","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Pang","name":"Pang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ling","familyName":"li","name":"li, Ling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhan","familyName":"Pu","name":"Pu, Yuhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaowei","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Zhaowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaobo","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Xiaobo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaheng","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Jiaheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OFFSIDE: Benchmarking Unlearning Misinformation in Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T05:05:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:51:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-03T06:29:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:12:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Advances in Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) intensify concerns about data privacy, making Machine Unlearning (MU), the selective removal of learned information, a critical necessity. However, existing MU benchmarks for MLLMs are limited by a lack of image diversity, potential inaccuracies, and insufficient evaluation scenarios, which fail to capture the complexity of real-world applications. To facilitate the development of MLLMs unlearning and alleviate the aforementioned limitations, we introduce OFFSIDE, a novel benchmark for evaluating misinformation unlearning in MLLMs based on football transfer rumors. This manually curated dataset contains 15.68K records for 80 players, providing a comprehensive framework with four test sets to assess forgetting efficacy, generalization, utility, and robustness. OFFSIDE supports advanced settings like selective unlearning and corrective relearning, and crucially, unimodal unlearning (forgetting only text data). Our extensive evaluation of multiple baselines reveals key findings: (1) Unimodal methods (erasing text-based knowledge) fail on multimodal rumors; (2) Unlearning efficacy is largely driven by catastrophic forgetting; (3) All methods struggle with \"visual rumors\" (rumors appear in the image); (4) The unlearned rumors can be easily recovered and (5) All methods are vulnerable to prompt attacks. These results expose significant vulnerabilities in current approaches, highlighting the need for more robust multimodal unlearning solutions. The code is available at https://github.com/zh121800/OFFSIDE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22535","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:38Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:11:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22969","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22969","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22969","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kechen","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Kechen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sinuo","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Sinuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rongpeng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Rongpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangming","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Xiangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yansha","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Yansha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ming","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Ming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhifeng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Conditional Diffusion Model with Mean Field Communication as Wireless Resource Allocation Planner"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multiagent Systems (cs.MA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:42:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-30T01:03:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:36:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In wireless communication systems, efficient and adaptive resource allocation plays a crucial role in enhancing overall Quality of Service (QoS). Compared to the conventional Model-Free Reinforcement Learning (MFRL) scheme, Model-Based RL (MBRL) first learns a generative world model for subsequent planning. The reuse of historical experience in MBRL promises more stable training behavior, yet its deployment in large-scale wireless networks remains challenging due to high-dimensional stochastic dynamics, strong inter-agent cooperation, and communication constraints. To overcome these challenges, we propose the Multi-Agent Conditional Diffusion Model Planner (MA-CDMP) for decentralized communication resource management. Built upon the Distributed Training with Decentralized Execution (DTDE) paradigm, MA-CDMP models each communication node as an autonomous agent and employs Diffusion Models (DMs) to capture and predict environment dynamics. Meanwhile, an inverse dynamics model guides action generation, thereby enhancing sample efficiency and policy scalability. Moreover, to approximate large-scale agent interactions, a Mean-Field (MF) mechanism is introduced as an assistance to the classifier in DMs. This design mitigates inter-agent non-stationarity and enhances cooperation with minimal communication overhead in distributed settings. We further theoretically establish an upper bound on the distributional approximation error introduced by the MF-based diffusion generation, guaranteeing convergence stability and reliable modeling of multi-agent stochastic dynamics. Extensive experiments demonstrate that MA-CDMP consistently outperforms existing MARL baselines in terms of average reward and QoS metrics, showcasing its scalability and practicality for real-world wireless network optimization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22969","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:52Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:48:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22171","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22171","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22171","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erum","familyName":"Mushtaq","name":"Mushtaq, Erum","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zalan","familyName":"Fabian","name":"Fabian, Zalan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yavuz Faruk","familyName":"Bakman","name":"Bakman, Yavuz Faruk","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anil","familyName":"Ramakrishna","name":"Ramakrishna, Anil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahdi","familyName":"Soltanolkotabi","name":"Soltanolkotabi, Mahdi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salman","familyName":"Avestimehr","name":"Avestimehr, Salman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"HARMONY: Hidden Activation Representations and Model Output-Aware Uncertainty Estimation for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T05:45:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:27:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T19:46:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-02T01:05:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncertainty Estimation (UE) plays a central role in quantifying the reliability of model outputs and reducing unsafe generations via selective prediction. In this regard, most existing probability-based UE approaches rely on predefined functions, aggregating token probabilities into a single UE score using heuristics such as length-normalization. However, these methods often fail to capture the complex relationships between generated tokens and struggle to identify biased probabilities often influenced by \\textbf{language priors}. Another line of research uses hidden representations of the model and trains simple MLP architectures to predict uncertainty. However, such functions often lose the intricate \\textbf{ inter-token dependencies}. While prior works show that hidden representations encode multimodal alignment signals, our work demonstrates that how these signals are processed has a significant impact on the UE performance. To effectively leverage these signals to identify inter-token dependencies, and vision-text alignment, we propose \\textbf{HARMONY} (Hidden Activation Representations and Model Output-Aware Uncertainty Estimation for Vision-Language Models), a novel UE framework that integrates generated tokens ('text'), model's uncertainty score at the output ('MaxProb'), and its internal belief on the visual understanding of the image and the generated token (captured by 'hidden representations') at token level via appropriate input mapping design and suitable architecture choice. Our experimental experiments across two open-ended VQA benchmarks (A-OKVQA, and VizWiz) and four state-of-the-art VLMs (LLaVA-7B, LLaVA-13B, InstructBLIP, and Qwen-VL) show that HARMONY consistently matches or surpasses existing approaches, achieving up to 5\\% improvement in AUROC and 9\\% in PRR."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22171","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:08:58Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:08:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-02T04:48:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23240","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23240","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23240","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carmine","familyName":"Zaccagnino","name":"Zaccagnino, Carmine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fabio","familyName":"Quattrini","name":"Quattrini, Fabio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vittorio","familyName":"Pippi","name":"Pippi, Vittorio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Silvia","familyName":"Cascianelli","name":"Cascianelli, Silvia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alessio","familyName":"Tonioni","name":"Tonioni, Alessio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rita","familyName":"Cucchiara","name":"Cucchiara, Rita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Autoregressive Styled Text Image Generation, but Make it Reliable"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T11:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:39:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-28T11:39:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T02:23:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generating faithful and readable styled text images (especially for Styled Handwritten Text generation - HTG) is an open problem with several possible applications across graphic design, document understanding, and image editing. A lot of research effort in this task is dedicated to developing strategies that reproduce the stylistic characteristics of a given writer, with promising results in terms of style fidelity and generalization achieved by the recently proposed Autoregressive Transformer paradigm for HTG. However, this method requires additional inputs, lacks a proper stop mechanism, and might end up in repetition loops, generating visual artifacts. In this work, we rethink the autoregressive formulation by framing HTG as a multimodal prompt-conditioned generation task, and tackle the content controllability issues by introducing special textual input tokens for better alignment with the visual ones. Moreover, we devise a Classifier-Free-Guidance-based strategy for our autoregressive model. Through extensive experimental validation, we demonstrate that our approach, dubbed Eruku, compared to previous solutions requires fewer inputs, generalizes better to unseen styles, and follows more faithfully the textual prompt, improving content adherence."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at WACV2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23240","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:34:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:34:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-01T04:23:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André G.","familyName":"Viveiros","name":"Viveiros, André G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Fernandes","name":"Fernandes, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saul","familyName":"Santos","name":"Santos, Saul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sonal","familyName":"Sannigrahi","name":"Sannigrahi, Sonal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanouil","familyName":"Zaranis","name":"Zaranis, Emmanouil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuno M.","familyName":"Guerreiro","name":"Guerreiro, Nuno M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Farajian","name":"Farajian, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pierre","familyName":"Colombo","name":"Colombo, Pierre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Graham","familyName":"Neubig","name":"Neubig, Graham","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André F. T.","familyName":"Martins","name":"Martins, André F. T.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TowerVision: Understanding and Improving Multilinguality in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.7; I.2.10; I.5.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"68T07, 68T45, 68T50","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T17:02:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T19:23:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:07:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-06T11:09:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-07T01:37:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite significant advances in vision-language models (VLMs), most existing work follows an English-centric design process, limiting their effectiveness in multilingual settings. In this work, we provide a comprehensive empirical study analyzing the impact of several multilingual design choices, such as training data composition, encoder selection, and text backbones. The result is TowerVision, a family of open multilingual VLMs for both image-text and video-text tasks, built upon the multilingual text-only model Tower+. TowerVision achieves competitive performance on multiple multimodal multilingual benchmarks and shows particular strength in culturally grounded tasks and multimodal translation. By incorporating visual and cultural context during fine-tuning, our models surpass existing approaches trained on substantially larger datasets, as demonstrated on ALM-Bench and Multi30K (image tasks) and ViMUL-Bench (video tasks). Alongside the models, we release VisionBlocks, a high-quality, curated vision-language dataset. Our findings highlight that multilingual vision-language training data substantially improves cross-lingual generalization -- both from high-resource to underrepresented languages and vice versa -- and that instruction-tuned LLMs are not always the optimal initialization point. To support further research, we publicly release all models, data, and training recipes."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 7 figures, submitted to arXiv October 2025. All models, datasets, and training code will be released at https://huggingface.co/collections/utter-project/towervision"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-07T03:07:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoyu","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Ruoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beier","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Beier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junzhi","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Junzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liangyu","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Liangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive Stochastic Coefficients for Accelerating Diffusion Sampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T12:53:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:41:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-31T06:49:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-03T01:29:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based generative processes, formulated as differential equation solving, frequently balance computational speed with sample quality. Our theoretical investigation of ODE- and SDE-based solvers reveals complementary weaknesses: ODE solvers accumulate irreducible gradient error along deterministic trajectories, while SDE methods suffer from amplified discretization errors when the step budget is limited. Building upon this insight, we introduce AdaSDE, a novel single-step SDE solver that aims to unify the efficiency of ODEs with the error resilience of SDEs. Specifically, we introduce a single per-step learnable coefficient, estimated via lightweight distillation, which dynamically regulates the error correction strength to accelerate diffusion sampling. Notably, our framework can be integrated with existing solvers to enhance their capabilities. Extensive experiments demonstrate state-of-the-art performance: at 5 NFE, AdaSDE achieves FID scores of 4.18 on CIFAR-10, 8.05 on FFHQ and 6.96 on LSUN Bedroom. Codes are available in https://github.com/WLU-wry02/AdaSDE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"To appear in NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:35:27Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:35:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-03T03:02:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23588","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23588","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23588","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guangting","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Guangting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Qinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fei","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhijie","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanyong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FARMER: Flow AutoRegressive Transformer over Pixels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:54:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:59:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-30T07:38:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-31T00:31:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Directly modeling the explicit likelihood of the raw data distribution is key topic in the machine learning area, which achieves the scaling successes in Large Language Models by autoregressive modeling. However, continuous AR modeling over visual pixel data suffer from extremely long sequences and high-dimensional spaces. In this paper, we present FARMER, a novel end-to-end generative framework that unifies Normalizing Flows (NF) and Autoregressive (AR) models for tractable likelihood estimation and high-quality image synthesis directly from raw pixels. FARMER employs an invertible autoregressive flow to transform images into latent sequences, whose distribution is modeled implicitly by an autoregressive model. To address the redundancy and complexity in pixel-level modeling, we propose a self-supervised dimension reduction scheme that partitions NF latent channels into informative and redundant groups, enabling more effective and efficient AR modeling. Furthermore, we design a one-step distillation scheme to significantly accelerate inference speed and introduce a resampling-based classifier-free guidance algorithm to boost image generation quality. Extensive experiments demonstrate that FARMER achieves competitive performance compared to existing pixel-based generative models while providing exact likelihoods and scalable training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Bytedance Seed Technical Report"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23588","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:42Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-31T02:12:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22439","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22439","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22439","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Vosoughi","name":"Vosoughi, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongyi","familyName":"Zang","name":"Zang, Yongyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qihui","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Qihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathan","familyName":"Paek","name":"Paek, Nathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Randal","familyName":"Leistikow","name":"Leistikow, Randal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenliang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chenliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PromptReverb: Multimodal Room Impulse Response Generation Through Latent Rectified Flow Matching"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6, H.5.5","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T21:38:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:44:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-29T15:42:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-30T00:56:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Room impulse response (RIR) generation remains a critical challenge for creating immersive virtual acoustic environments. Current methods suffer from two fundamental limitations: the scarcity of full-band RIR datasets and the inability of existing models to generate acoustically accurate responses from diverse input modalities. We present PromptReverb, a two-stage generative framework that addresses these challenges. Our approach combines a variational autoencoder that upsamples band-limited RIRs to full-band quality (48 kHz), and a conditional diffusion transformer model based on rectified flow matching that generates RIRs from descriptions in natural language. Empirical evaluation demonstrates that PromptReverb produces RIRs with superior perceptual quality and acoustic accuracy compared to existing methods, achieving 8.8% mean RT60 error compared to -37% for widely used baselines and yielding more realistic room-acoustic parameters. Our method enables practical applications in virtual reality, architectural acoustics, and audio production where flexible, high-quality RIR synthesis is essential."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 2 figures, 4 tables; v2: corrected spelling of a co-author name; no content changes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22439","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-30T02:19:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongkai","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Hongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingkang","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Dingkang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyang","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Mingyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"More Than Generation: Unifying Generation and Depth Estimation via Text-to-Image Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:44:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T15:40:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Generative depth estimation methods leverage the rich visual priors stored in pre-trained text-to-image diffusion models, demonstrating astonishing zero-shot capability. However, parameter updates during training lead to catastrophic degradation in the image generation capability of the pre-trained model. We introduce MERGE, a unified model for image generation and depth estimation, starting from a fixed pre-trained text-to-image model. MERGE demonstrates that the pre-trained text-to-image model can do more than image generation, but also expand to depth estimation effortlessly. Specifically, MERGE introduces a play-and-plug framework that enables seamless switching between image generation and depth estimation modes through simple and pluggable converters. Meanwhile, we propose a Group Reuse Mechanism to encourage parameter reuse and improve the utilization of the additional learnable parameters. MERGE unleashes the powerful depth estimation capability of the pre-trained text-to-image model while preserving its original image generation ability. Compared to other unified models for image generation and depth estimation, MERGE achieves state-of-the-art performance across multiple depth estimation benchmarks. The code will be made available at https://github.com/H-EmbodVis/MERGE"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025. The code will be made available at https://github.com/H-EmbodVis/MERGE"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:42:22Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:42:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:26:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Odin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Odin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haitao","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Haitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinghan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qinghan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuanle","familyName":"Mo","name":"Mo, Yuanle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiantai","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Qiantai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gang","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Gang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zichang","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zichang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyi","familyName":"You","name":"You, Ziyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peicong","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Peicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijie","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yijie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyang","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Yuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shicheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Shicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jack Xiaoyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Jack Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenqing","familyName":"Hua","name":"Hua, Chenqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weibo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Weibo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runze","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Runze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunpeng","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yunpeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejun","familyName":"Ying","name":"Ying, Kejun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yundian","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yundian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Lang","name":"Lang, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peichen","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Peichen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanqun","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Hanqun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Qiang","name":"Qiang, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaqi","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jiaqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengfei","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Pengfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang-yu","familyName":"Hsieh","name":"Hsieh, Chang-yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pheng Ann","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pheng Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siqi","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Siqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tingjun","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Tingjun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuangjia","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shuangjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ODesign: A World Model for Biomolecular Interaction Design"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Biomolecules (q-bio.BM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T14:16:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:37:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T09:13:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-29T00:40:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Biomolecular interactions underpin almost all biological processes, and their rational design is central to programming new biological functions. Generative AI models have emerged as powerful tools for molecular design, yet most remain specialized for individual molecular types and lack fine-grained control over interaction details. Here we present ODesign, an all-atom generative world model for all-to-all biomolecular interaction design. ODesign allows scientists to specify epitopes on arbitrary targets and generate diverse classes of binding partners with fine-grained control. Across entity-, token-, and atom-level benchmarks in the protein modality, ODesign demonstrates superior controllability and performance to modality-specific baselines. Extending beyond proteins, it generalizes to nucleic acid and small-molecule design, enabling interaction types such as protein-binding RNA/DNA and RNA/DNA-binding ligands that were previously inaccessible. By unifying multimodal biomolecular interactions within a single generative framework, ODesign moves toward a general-purpose molecular world model capable of programmable design. ODesign is available at https://odesign.lglab.ac.cn ,"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:12:11Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:12:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-29T02:25:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23606","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23606","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23606","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Schwing","name":"Schwing, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhizhen","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zhizhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Variational Masked Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T17:59:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T02:00:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Masked diffusion models have recently emerged as a flexible framework for discrete generative modeling. However, a key limitation of standard masked diffusion is its inability to effectively capture dependencies among tokens that are predicted concurrently, leading to degraded generation quality when dependencies among tokens are important. To explicitly model dependencies among tokens, we propose Variational Masked Diffusion (VMD), a framework that introduces latent variables into the masked diffusion process. Through controlled experiments on synthetic datasets, we demonstrate that VMD successfully learns dependencies that conventional masked diffusion fails to capture. We further validate the effectiveness of our approach on Sudoku puzzles and text datasets, where learning of dependencies among tokens improves global consistency. Across these domains, VMD enhances both generation quality and dependency awareness, highlighting the value of integrating variational inference into masked diffusion. Our code is available at: https://riccizz.github.io/VMD."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project Page: https://riccizz.github.io/VMD"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23606","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:43:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:43:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:43:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23511","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23511","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23511","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erjin","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Erjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fan","familyName":"Jia","name":"Jia, Fan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoqiang","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Haoqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haowei","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hebei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hebei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianjian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jianjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Bin","name":"Bin, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junwen","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Junwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixin","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Kaixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kefan","familyName":"Gu","name":"Gu, Kefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lin","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Lin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peilong","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Peilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruitao","familyName":"Hao","name":"Hao, Ruitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruitao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ruitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saike","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Saike","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songhan","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Songhan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tiancai","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Tiancai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianle","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Tianle","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenbin","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Wenbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqi","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Wenqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingfei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yingfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yizhuang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Yizhuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yucheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunchao","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Yunchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfei","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Yunfei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yuxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ze","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeming","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zeming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziheng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Ziming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziwei","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Ziwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ziyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dexbotic: Open-Source Vision-Language-Action Toolbox"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T16:47:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:54:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we present Dexbotic, an open-source Vision-Language-Action (VLA) model toolbox based on PyTorch. It aims to provide a one-stop VLA research service for professionals in the field of embodied intelligence. It offers a codebase that supports multiple mainstream VLA policies simultaneously, allowing users to reproduce various VLA methods with just a single environment setup. The toolbox is experiment-centric, where the users can quickly develop new VLA experiments by simply modifying the Exp script. Moreover, we provide much stronger pretrained models to achieve great performance improvements for state-of-the-art VLA policies. Dexbotic will continuously update to include more of the latest pre-trained foundation models and cutting-edge VLA models in the industry."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Authors are listed in alphabetical order. The official website is located at https://dexbotic.com/. Code is available at https://github.com/Dexmal/dexbotic"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23511","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:40:50Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:40:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:40:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23382","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23382","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23382","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Songxi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Songxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tang","familyName":"Sui","name":"Sui, Tang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qunying","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Qunying","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An Efficient Remote Sensing Super Resolution Method Exploring Diffusion Priors and Multi-Modal Constraints for Crop Type Mapping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T14:34:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:47:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Super resolution offers a way to harness medium even lowresolution but historically valuable remote sensing image archives. Generative models, especially diffusion models, have recently been applied to remote sensing super resolution (RSSR), yet several challenges exist. First, diffusion models are effective but require expensive training from scratch resources and have slow inference speeds. Second, current methods have limited utilization of auxiliary information as real-world constraints to reconstruct scientifically realistic images. Finally, most current methods lack evaluation on downstream tasks. In this study, we present a efficient LSSR framework for RSSR, supported by a new multimodal dataset of paired 30 m Landsat 8 and 10 m Sentinel 2 imagery. Built on frozen pretrained Stable Diffusion, LSSR integrates crossmodal attention with auxiliary knowledge (Digital Elevation Model, land cover, month) and Synthetic Aperture Radar guidance, enhanced by adapters and a tailored Fourier NDVI loss to balance spatial details and spectral fidelity. Extensive experiments demonstrate that LSSR significantly improves crop boundary delineation and recovery, achieving state-of-the-art performance with Peak Signal-to-Noise Ratio/Structural Similarity Index Measure of 32.63/0.84 (RGB) and 23.99/0.78 (IR), and the lowest NDVI Mean Squared Error (0.042), while maintaining efficient inference (0.39 sec/image). Moreover, LSSR transfers effectively to NASA Harmonized Landsat and Sentinel (HLS) super resolution, yielding more reliable crop classification (F1: 0.86) than Sentinel-2 (F1: 0.85). These results highlight the potential of RSSR to advance precision agriculture."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"41 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23382","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:37:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:37:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:37:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23184","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23184","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23184","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junho","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Junho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Young Min","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Young Min","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Finding 3D Scene Analogies with Multimodal Foundation Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T10:23:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:34:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Connecting current observations with prior experiences helps robots adapt and plan in new, unseen 3D environments. Recently, 3D scene analogies have been proposed to connect two 3D scenes, which are smooth maps that align scene regions with common spatial relationships. These maps enable detailed transfer of trajectories or waypoints, potentially supporting demonstration transfer for imitation learning or task plan transfer across scenes. However, existing methods for the task require additional training and fixed object vocabularies. In this work, we propose to use multimodal foundation models for finding 3D scene analogies in a zero-shot, open-vocabulary setting. Central to our approach is a hybrid neural representation of scenes that consists of a sparse graph based on vision-language model features and a feature field derived from 3D shape foundation models. 3D scene analogies are then found in a coarse-to-fine manner, by first aligning the graph and refining the correspondence with feature fields. Our method can establish accurate correspondences between complex scenes, and we showcase applications in trajectory and waypoint transfer."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to FM4RoboPlan workshop at RSS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23184","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:33:03Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:33:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:33:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23066","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23066","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23066","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yichao","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Yichao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushuo","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yushuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qishuai","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Qishuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kent Chiu","familyName":"Jin-Chun","name":"Jin-Chun, Kent Chiu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenneth Zhu","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Kenneth Zhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Donald","familyName":"MacDonald","name":"MacDonald, Donald","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Stage Field Extraction of Financial Documents with OCR and Compact Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:56:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:25:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Financial documents are essential sources of information for regulators, auditors, and financial institutions, particularly for assessing the wealth and compliance of Small and Medium-sized Businesses. However, SMB documents are often difficult to parse. They are rarely born digital and instead are distributed as scanned images that are none machine readable. The scans themselves are low in resolution, affected by skew or rotation, and often contain noisy backgrounds. These documents also tend to be heterogeneous, mixing narratives, tables, figures, and multilingual content within the same report. Such characteristics pose major challenges for automated information extraction, especially when relying on end to end large Vision Language Models, which are computationally expensive, sensitive to noise, and slow when applied to files with hundreds of pages.\n We propose a multistage pipeline that leverages traditional image processing models and OCR extraction, together with compact VLMs for structured field extraction of large-scale financial documents. Our approach begins with image pre-processing, including segmentation, orientation detection, and size normalization. Multilingual OCR is then applied to recover page-level text. Upon analyzing the text information, pages are retrieved for coherent sections. Finally, compact VLMs are operated within these narrowed-down scopes to extract structured financial indicators.\n Our approach is evaluated using an internal corpus of multi-lingual, scanned financial documents. The results demonstrate that compact VLMs, together with a multistage pipeline, achieves 8.8 times higher field level accuracy relative to directly feeding the whole document into large VLMs, only at 0.7 percent of the GPU cost and 92.6 percent less end-to-end service latency."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23066","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:30:12Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:30:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:30:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23040","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23040","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23040","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Subhojyoti","familyName":"Khastagir","name":"Khastagir, Subhojyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kishalay","familyName":"Das","name":"Das, Kishalay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pawan","familyName":"Goyal","name":"Goyal, Pawan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seung-Cheol","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Seung-Cheol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satadeep","familyName":"Bhattacharjee","name":"Bhattacharjee, Satadeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloy","familyName":"Ganguly","name":"Ganguly, Niloy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Meets Diffusion: A Hybrid Framework for Crystal Material Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Materials Science (cond-mat.mtrl-sci)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T06:08:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:24:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in generative modeling have shown significant promise in designing novel periodic crystal structures. Existing approaches typically rely on either large language models (LLMs) or equivariant denoising models, each with complementary strengths: LLMs excel at handling discrete atomic types but often struggle with continuous features such as atomic positions and lattice parameters, while denoising models are effective at modeling continuous variables but encounter difficulties in generating accurate atomic compositions. To bridge this gap, we propose CrysLLMGen, a hybrid framework that integrates an LLM with a diffusion model to leverage their complementary strengths for crystal material generation. During sampling, CrysLLMGen first employs a fine-tuned LLM to produce an intermediate representation of atom types, atomic coordinates, and lattice structure. While retaining the predicted atom types, it passes the atomic coordinates and lattice structure to a pre-trained equivariant diffusion model for refinement. Our framework outperforms state-of-the-art generative models across several benchmark tasks and datasets. Specifically, CrysLLMGen not only achieves a balanced performance in terms of structural and compositional validity but also generates more stable and novel materials compared to LLM-based and denoisingbased models Furthermore, CrysLLMGen exhibits strong conditional generation capabilities, effectively producing materials that satisfy user-defined constraints. Code is available at https://github.com/kdmsit/crysllmgen"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23040","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:35Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:29:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.23032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.23032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen-Che","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Chen-Che","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun-Cheng","familyName":"Chou","name":"Chou, Yun-Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Teng-Ruei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Teng-Ruei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"P1GPT: a multi-agent LLM workflow module for multi-modal financial information analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Engineering, Finance, and Science (cs.CE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T05:52:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:23:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled multi-agent reasoning systems capable of collaborative decision-making. However, in financial analysis, most frameworks remain narrowly focused on either isolated single-agent predictors or loosely connected analyst ensembles, and they lack a coherent reasoning workflow that unifies diverse data modalities. We introduce P1GPT, a layered multi-agent LLM framework for multi-modal financial information analysis and interpretable trading decision support. Unlike prior systems that emulate trading teams through role simulation, P1GPT implements a structured reasoning pipeline that systematically fuses technical, fundamental, and news-based insights through coordinated agent communication and integration-time synthesis. Backtesting on multi-modal datasets across major U.S. equities demonstrates that P1GPT achieves superior cumulative and risk-adjusted returns, maintains low drawdowns, and provides transparent causal rationales. These findings suggest that structured reasoning workflows, rather than agent role imitation, offer a scalable path toward explainable and trustworthy financial AI systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.23032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:29:23Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:29:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:29:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22964","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22964","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22964","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liling","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Liling","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ning","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Yue","name":"Yue, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yidan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yidan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedram","familyName":"Ghamisi","name":"Ghamisi, Pedram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antonio","familyName":"Plaza","name":"Plaza, Antonio","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leyuan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Leyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Survey of Multimodal Geospatial Foundation Models: Techniques, Applications, and Challenges"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T03:40:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:19:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Foundation models have transformed natural language processing and computer vision, and their impact is now reshaping remote sensing image analysis. With powerful generalization and transfer learning capabilities, they align naturally with the multimodal, multi-resolution, and multi-temporal characteristics of remote sensing data. To address unique challenges in the field, multimodal geospatial foundation models (GFMs) have emerged as a dedicated research frontier. This survey delivers a comprehensive review of multimodal GFMs from a modality-driven perspective, covering five core visual and vision-language modalities. We examine how differences in imaging physics and data representation shape interaction design, and we analyze key techniques for alignment, integration, and knowledge transfer to tackle modality heterogeneity, distribution shifts, and semantic gaps. Advances in training paradigms, architectures, and task-specific adaptation strategies are systematically assessed alongside a wealth of emerging benchmarks. Representative multimodal visual and vision-language GFMs are evaluated across ten downstream tasks, with insights into their architectures, performance, and application scenarios. Real-world case studies, spanning land cover mapping, agricultural monitoring, disaster response, climate studies, and geospatial intelligence, demonstrate the practical potential of GFMs. Finally, we outline pressing challenges in domain generalization, interpretability, efficiency, and privacy, and chart promising avenues for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22964","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:27:45Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:27:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:27:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22928","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22928","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22928","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingze","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Mingze","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jianbang","familyName":"You","name":"You, Jianbang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diffuse to Detect: A Generalizable Framework for Anomaly Detection with Diffusion Models Applications to UAVs and Beyond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-27T02:08:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:16:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Anomaly detection in complex, high-dimensional data, such as UAV sensor readings, is essential for operational safety but challenging for existing methods due to their limited sensitivity, scalability, and inability to capture intricate dependencies. We propose the Diffuse to Detect (DTD) framework, a novel approach that innovatively adapts diffusion models for anomaly detection, diverging from their conventional use in generative tasks with high inference time. By comparison, DTD employs a single-step diffusion process to predict noise patterns, enabling rapid and precise identification of anomalies without reconstruction errors. This approach is grounded in robust theoretical foundations that link noise prediction to the data distribution's score function, ensuring reliable deviation detection. By integrating Graph Neural Networks to model sensor relationships as dynamic graphs, DTD effectively captures spatial (inter-sensor) and temporal anomalies. Its two-branch architecture, with parametric neural network-based energy scoring for scalability and nonparametric statistical methods for interpretability, provides flexible trade-offs between computational efficiency and transparency. Extensive evaluations on UAV sensor data, multivariate time series, and images demonstrate DTD's superior performance over existing methods, underscoring its generality across diverse data modalities. This versatility, combined with its adaptability, positions DTD as a transformative solution for safety-critical applications, including industrial monitoring and beyond."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22928","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:26:54Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:26:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:26:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22852","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22852","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marianne","familyName":"Arriola","name":"Arriola, Marianne","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yair","familyName":"Schiff","name":"Schiff, Yair","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Phung","name":"Phung, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aaron","familyName":"Gokaslan","name":"Gokaslan, Aaron","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Volodymyr","familyName":"Kuleshov","name":"Kuleshov, Volodymyr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Encoder-Decoder Diffusion Language Models for Efficient Training and Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T22:05:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Discrete diffusion models enable parallel token sampling for faster inference than autoregressive approaches. However, prior diffusion models use a decoder-only architecture, which requires sampling algorithms that invoke the full network at every denoising step and incur high computational cost. Our key insight is that discrete diffusion models perform two types of computation: 1) representing clean tokens and 2) denoising corrupted tokens, which enables us to use separate modules for each task. We propose an encoder-decoder architecture to accelerate discrete diffusion inference, which relies on an encoder to represent clean tokens and a lightweight decoder to iteratively refine a noised sequence. We also show that this architecture enables faster training of block diffusion models, which partition sequences into blocks for better quality and are commonly used in diffusion language model inference. We introduce a framework for Efficient Encoder-Decoder Diffusion (E2D2), consisting of an architecture with specialized training and sampling algorithms, and we show that E2D2 achieves superior trade-offs between generation quality and inference throughput on summarization, translation, and mathematical reasoning tasks. We provide the code, model weights, and blog post on the project page: https://m-arriola.com/e2d2"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025. We provide the code at https://github.com/kuleshov-group/e2d2"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22852","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22851","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22851","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22851","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lexiang","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Lexiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Chengyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingwen","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Jingwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuecong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yuecong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic Surgery: Zero-Shot Concept Erasure in Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T22:04:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:11:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Concept erasure in text-to-image diffusion models is crucial for mitigating harmful content, yet existing methods often compromise generative quality. We introduce Semantic Surgery, a novel training-free, zero-shot framework for concept erasure that operates directly on text embeddings before the diffusion process. It dynamically estimates the presence of target concepts in a prompt and performs a calibrated vector subtraction to neutralize their influence at the source, enhancing both erasure completeness and locality. The framework includes a Co-Occurrence Encoding module for robust multi-concept erasure and a visual feedback loop to address latent concept persistence. As a training-free method, Semantic Surgery adapts dynamically to each prompt, ensuring precise interventions. Extensive experiments on object, explicit content, artistic style, and multi-celebrity erasure tasks show our method significantly outperforms state-of-the-art approaches. We achieve superior completeness and robustness while preserving locality and image quality (e.g., 93.58 H-score in object erasure, reducing explicit content to just 1 instance, and 8.09 H_a in style erasure with no quality degradation). This robustness also allows our framework to function as a built-in threat detection system, offering a practical solution for safer text-to-image generation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025). Code is available at https://github.com/Lexiang-Xiong/Semantic-Surgery"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22851","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:25:05Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:25:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:25:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22838","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22838","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22838","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aya","familyName":"Nakayama","name":"Nakayama, Aya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Wong","name":"Wong, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Nishimura","name":"Nishimura, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaito","familyName":"Tanaka","name":"Tanaka, Kaito","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Semantic-Preserving Cross-Style Visual Reasoning for Robust Multi-Modal Understanding in Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T21:11:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:10:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The \"style trap\" poses a significant challenge for Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), hindering robust semantic understanding across diverse visual styles, especially in in-context learning (ICL). Existing methods often fail to effectively decouple style from content, hindering generalization. To address this, we propose the Semantic-Preserving Cross-Style Visual Reasoner (SP-CSVR), a novel framework for stable semantic understanding and adaptive cross-style visual reasoning. SP-CSVR integrates a Cross-Style Feature Encoder (CSFE) for style-content disentanglement, a Semantic-Aligned In-Context Decoder (SAICD) for efficient few-shot style adaptation, and an Adaptive Semantic Consistency Module (ASCM) employing multi-task contrastive learning to enforce cross-style semantic invariance. Extensive experiments on a challenging multi-style dataset demonstrate SP-CSVR's state-of-the-art performance across visual captioning, visual question answering, and in-context style adaptation. Comprehensive evaluations, including ablation studies and generalization analysis, confirm SP-CSVR's efficacy in enhancing robustness, generalization, and efficiency across diverse visual styles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22838","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:24:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22798","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22798","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22798","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thu Phuong","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Thu Phuong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Duc M.","familyName":"Nguyen","name":"Nguyen, Duc M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyotaek","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hyotaek","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunwook","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Hyunwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyunmin","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Hyunmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungahn","familyName":"Ko","name":"Ko, Sungahn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taehwan","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Taehwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VEHME: A Vision-Language Model For Evaluating Handwritten Mathematics Expressions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T19:03:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:08:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automatically assessing handwritten mathematical solutions is an important problem in educational technology with practical applications, but it remains a significant challenge due to the diverse formats, unstructured layouts, and symbolic complexity of student work. To address this challenge, we introduce VEHME-a Vision-Language Model for Evaluating Handwritten Mathematics Expressions-designed to assess open-form handwritten math responses with high accuracy and interpretable reasoning traces. VEHME integrates a two-phase training pipeline: (i) supervised fine-tuning using structured reasoning data, and (ii) reinforcement learning that aligns model outputs with multi-dimensional grading objectives, including correctness, reasoning depth, and error localization. To enhance spatial understanding, we propose an Expression-Aware Visual Prompting Module, trained on our synthesized multi-line math expressions dataset to robustly guide attention in visually heterogeneous inputs. Evaluated on AIHub and FERMAT datasets, VEHME achieves state-of-the-art performance among open-source models and approaches the accuracy of proprietary systems, demonstrating its potential as a scalable and accessible tool for automated math assessment. Our training and experiment code is publicly available at our GitHub repository."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025. Project Website: https://vehme.github.io/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22798","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:51Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:23:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22785","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22785","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22785","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiaxiang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiaxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prayag","familyName":"Tiwari","name":"Tiwari, Prayag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingkun","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Mingkun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self-Calibrated Consistency can Fight Back for Adversarial Robustness in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T18:37:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:07:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pre-trained vision-language models (VLMs) such as CLIP have demonstrated strong zero-shot capabilities across diverse domains, yet remain highly vulnerable to adversarial perturbations that disrupt image-text alignment and compromise reliability. Existing defenses typically rely on adversarial fine-tuning with labeled data, limiting their applicability in zero-shot settings. In this work, we identify two key weaknesses of current CLIP adversarial attacks -- lack of semantic guidance and vulnerability to view variations -- collectively termed semantic and viewpoint fragility. To address these challenges, we propose Self-Calibrated Consistency (SCC), an effective test-time defense. SCC consists of two complementary modules: Semantic consistency, which leverages soft pseudo-labels from counterattack warm-up and multi-view predictions to regularize cross-modal alignment and separate the target embedding from confusable negatives; and Spatial consistency, aligning perturbed visual predictions via augmented views to stabilize inference under adversarial perturbations. Together, these modules form a plug-and-play inference strategy. Extensive experiments on 22 benchmarks under diverse attack settings show that SCC consistently improves the zero-shot robustness of CLIP while maintaining accuracy, and can be seamlessly integrated with other VLMs for further gains. These findings highlight the great potential of establishing an adversarially robust paradigm from CLIP, with implications extending to broader vision-language domains such as BioMedCLIP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22785","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:23:33Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:23:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:23:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22751","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22751","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22751","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piyushkumar","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Piyushkumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Modal Fact-Verification Framework for Reducing Hallucinations in Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T16:58:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:06:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Large Language Models have transformed how we interact with AI systems, they suffer from a critical flaw: they confidently generate false information that sounds entirely plausible. This hallucination problem has become a major barrier to deploying these models in real-world applications where accuracy matters. We developed a fact verification framework that catches and corrects these errors in real-time by cross checking LLM outputs against multiple knowledge sources. Our system combines structured databases, live web searches, and academic literature to verify factual claims as they're generated. When we detect inconsistencies, we automatically correct them while preserving the natural flow of the response. Testing across various domains showed we could reduce hallucinations by 67% without sacrificing response quality. Domain experts in healthcare, finance, and scientific research rated our corrected outputs 89% satisfactory a significant improvement over unverified LLM responses. This work offers a practical solution for making LLMs more trustworthy in applications where getting facts wrong isn't an option."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22751","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:22:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:22:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:22:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22694","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22694","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22694","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Shu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianyi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Tianyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilesh","familyName":"Ahuja","name":"Ahuja, Nilesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omesh","familyName":"Tickoo","name":"Tickoo, Omesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijaykrishnan","familyName":"Narayanan","name":"Narayanan, Vijaykrishnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Windsock is Dancing: Adaptive Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T14:36:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:03:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Retrieval-Augmented Generation (MRAG) has emerged as a promising method to generate factual and up-to-date responses of Multimodal Large Language Models (MLLMs) by incorporating non-parametric knowledge from external knowledge bases. However, existing MRAG approaches suffer from static retrieval strategies, inflexible modality selection, and suboptimal utilization of retrieved information, leading to three critical challenges: determining when to retrieve, what modality to incorporate, and how to utilize retrieved information effectively. To address these challenges, we introduce Windsock, a query-dependent module making decisions on retrieval necessity and modality selection, effectively reducing computational overhead and improving response quality. Additionally, we propose Dynamic Noise-Resistance (DANCE) Instruction Tuning, an adaptive training strategy that enhances MLLMs' ability to utilize retrieved information while maintaining robustness against noise. Moreover, we adopt a self-assessment approach leveraging knowledge within MLLMs to convert question-answering datasets to MRAG training datasets. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our proposed method significantly improves the generation quality by 17.07% while reducing 8.95% retrieval times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 UniReps Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22694","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:21:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:21:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:21:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22691","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22691","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22691","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruslan","familyName":"Berdichevsky","name":"Berdichevsky, Ruslan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shai","familyName":"Nahum-Gefen","name":"Nahum-Gefen, Shai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elad Ben","familyName":"Zaken","name":"Zaken, Elad Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SALSA: Single-pass Autoregressive LLM Structured Classification"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T14:28:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:02:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite their impressive generalization capabilities, instruction-tuned Large Language Models often underperform on text classification benchmarks. We introduce SALSA, a coherent pipeline that combines structured prompting, class-to-token mapping, and parameter-efficient fine-tuning, thereby avoiding cold-start training. Each class label is mapped to a distinct output token, and prompts are constructed to elicit a single-token response. During inference, the model's output is projected only onto the logits of the relevant class tokens, enabling efficient and accurate classification in a single forward pass. SALSA achieves state-of-the-art results across diverse benchmarks, demonstrating its robustness and scalability for LLM-based classification applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22691","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:21:20Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:21:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:21:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22641","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22641","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22641","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Divya Jyoti","familyName":"Bajpai","name":"Bajpai, Divya Jyoti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manjesh Kumar","familyName":"Hanawal","name":"Hanawal, Manjesh Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FastVLM: Self-Speculative Decoding for Fast Vision-Language Model Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T11:49:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:59:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-language Models (VLMs) have made significant strides in visual understanding and query response generation, but often face challenges of high computational cost and inference latency due to autoregressive decoding. In this work, we introduce an imitation-learning-based Self-Speculative Decoding (SSD) framework, named FastVLM, to address these limitations. Our approach employs a lightweight draft model for token generation in an autoregressive manner, while a full model verifies these tokens non-autoregressively. Accepted tokens proceed seamlessly, while rejected tokens are corrected by the full model and used to guide the draft model's refinement. Through an imitation network, FastVLM enhances the draft model by integrating deeper level insights from the full model's architecture. Also, it maintains the performance integrity of the full model while training the draft model, achieving a balance between efficiency and accuracy. Our method speeds up the inference process by 1.55-1.85x as compared to the final layer with minimal loss in performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for presentation at the main Conference IJCNLP-AACL 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22641","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:20:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22622","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22622","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22622","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kangran","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Kangran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yupeng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yupeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yize","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yize","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weinan","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Weinan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baicheng","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Baicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhe","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Chengzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soumyya Kanti","familyName":"Datta","name":"Datta, Soumyya Kanti","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qingshan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Qingshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siwei","familyName":"Lyu","name":"Lyu, Siwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baoyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Baoyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DeepfakeBench-MM: A Comprehensive Benchmark for Multimodal Deepfake Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T10:40:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:57:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The misuse of advanced generative AI models has resulted in the widespread proliferation of falsified data, particularly forged human-centric audiovisual content, which poses substantial societal risks (e.g., financial fraud and social instability). In response to this growing threat, several works have preliminarily explored countermeasures. However, the lack of sufficient and diverse training data, along with the absence of a standardized benchmark, hinder deeper exploration. To address this challenge, we first build Mega-MMDF, a large-scale, diverse, and high-quality dataset for multimodal deepfake detection. Specifically, we employ 21 forgery pipelines through the combination of 10 audio forgery methods, 12 visual forgery methods, and 6 audio-driven face reenactment methods. Mega-MMDF currently contains 0.1 million real samples and 1.1 million forged samples, making it one of the largest and most diverse multimodal deepfake datasets, with plans for continuous expansion. Building on it, we present DeepfakeBench-MM, the first unified benchmark for multimodal deepfake detection. It establishes standardized protocols across the entire detection pipeline and serves as a versatile platform for evaluating existing methods as well as exploring novel approaches. DeepfakeBench-MM currently supports 5 datasets and 11 multimodal deepfake detectors. Furthermore, our comprehensive evaluations and in-depth analyses uncover several key findings from multiple perspectives (e.g., augmentation, stacked forgery). We believe that DeepfakeBench-MM, together with our large-scale Mega-MMDF, will serve as foundational infrastructures for advancing multimodal deepfake detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Preprint"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22622","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:19:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:19:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:19:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22571","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22571","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22571","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahiro","familyName":"Ukai","name":"Ukai, Mahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuhei","familyName":"Kurita","name":"Kurita, Shuhei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nakamasa","familyName":"Inoue","name":"Inoue, Nakamasa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"STATUS Bench: A Rigorous Benchmark for Evaluating Object State Understanding in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T08:04:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:54:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Object state recognition aims to identify the specific condition of objects, such as their positional states (e.g., open or closed) and functional states (e.g., on or off). While recent Vision-Language Models (VLMs) are capable of performing a variety of multimodal tasks, it remains unclear how precisely they can identify object states. To alleviate this issue, we introduce the STAte and Transition UnderStanding Benchmark (STATUS Bench), the first benchmark for rigorously evaluating the ability of VLMs to understand subtle variations in object states in diverse situations. Specifically, STATUS Bench introduces a novel evaluation scheme that requires VLMs to perform three tasks simultaneously: object state identification (OSI), image retrieval (IR), and state change identification (SCI). These tasks are defined over our fully hand-crafted dataset involving image pairs, their corresponding object state descriptions and state change descriptions. Furthermore, we introduce a large-scale training dataset, namely STATUS Train, which consists of 13 million semi-automatically created descriptions. This dataset serves as the largest resource to facilitate further research in this area. In our experiments, we demonstrate that STATUS Bench enables rigorous consistency evaluation and reveal that current state-of-the-art VLMs still significantly struggle to capture subtle object state distinctions. Surprisingly, under the proposed rigorous evaluation scheme, most open-weight VLMs exhibited chance-level zero-shot performance. After fine-tuning on STATUS Train, Qwen2.5-VL achieved performance comparable to Gemini 2.0 Flash. These findings underscore the necessity of STATUS Bench and Train for advancing object state recognition in VLM research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22571","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:18:29Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:18:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:18:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22527","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22527","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22527","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morgan","familyName":"Himes","name":"Himes, Morgan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samiksha","familyName":"Krishnamurthy","name":"Krishnamurthy, Samiksha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Lizarraga","name":"Lizarraga, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Srinath","familyName":"Saikrishnan","name":"Saikrishnan, Srinath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vikram","familyName":"Seenivasan","name":"Seenivasan, Vikram","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonathan","familyName":"Soriano","name":"Soriano, Jonathan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ying Nian","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ying Nian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuan","familyName":"Do","name":"Do, Tuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Modal Masked Autoencoders for Learning Image-Spectrum Associations for Galaxy Evolution and Cosmology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Astrophysics of Galaxies (astro-ph.GA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T04:29:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:50:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Upcoming surveys will produce billions of galaxy images but comparatively few spectra, motivating models that learn cross-modal representations. We build a dataset of 134,533 galaxy images (HSC-PDR2) and spectra (DESI-DR1) and adapt a Multi-Modal Masked Autoencoder (MMAE) to embed both images and spectra in a shared representation. The MMAE is a transformer-based architecture, which we train by masking 75% of the data and reconstructing missing image and spectral tokens. We use this model to test three applications: spectral and image reconstruction from heavily masked data and redshift regression from images alone. It recovers key physical features, such as galaxy shapes, atomic emission line peaks, and broad continuum slopes, though it struggles with fine image details and line strengths. For redshift regression, the MMAE performs comparably or better than prior multi-modal models in terms of prediction scatter even when missing spectra in testing. These results highlight both the potential and limitations of masked autoencoders in astrophysics and motivate extensions to additional modalities, such as text, for foundation models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 3 figures, 1 table, accepted to NeurIPS 2025 Workshop ML4PS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22527","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:26Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:17:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22515","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22515","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22515","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jing-An","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jing-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Li","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Li","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles","familyName":"Gale","name":"Gale, Charles","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangyong","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Sangyong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"An End-to-End Generative Diffusion Model for Heavy-Ion Collisions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Theory (nucl-th)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-26T03:39:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:49:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Heavy-ion collision physics has entered the high precision era, demanding theoretical models capable of generating huge statistics to compare with experimental data. However, traditional hybrid models, which combine hydrodynamics and hadronic transport, are computationally intensive, creating a significant bottleneck. In this work, we introduce DiffHIC, an end-to-end generative diffusion model, to emulate ultra-relativistic heavy-ion collisions. The model takes initial entropy density profiles and transport coefficients as input and directly generates two-dimensional final-state particle spectra. Our results demonstrate that DiffHIC achieves a computational speedup of approximately $10^5$ against traditional simulations, while accurately reproducing a wide range of physical observables, including integrated and differential anisotropic flow, multi-particle correlations, and momentum fluctuations. This framework provides a powerful and efficient tool for phenomenological studies in the high-precision era of heavy-ion physics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Contribution to: Quark Matter 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22515","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:17:10Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:17:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:17:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22455","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22455","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22455","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brandon James","familyName":"Carone","name":"Carone, Brandon James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iran R.","familyName":"Roman","name":"Roman, Iran R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pablo","familyName":"Ripollés","name":"Ripollés, Pablo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Multimodal Large Language Models on Core Music Perception Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T23:10:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:46:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal Large Language Models (LLMs) claim \"musical understanding\" via evaluations that conflate listening with score reading. We benchmark three SOTA LLMs (Gemini 2.5 Pro, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and Qwen2.5-Omni) across three core music skills: Syncopation Scoring, Transposition Detection, and Chord Quality Identification. Moreover, we separate three sources of variability: (i) perceptual limitations (audio vs. MIDI inputs), (ii) exposure to examples (zero- vs. few-shot manipulations), and (iii) reasoning strategies (Standalone, CoT, LogicLM). For the latter we adapt LogicLM, a framework combining LLMs with symbolic solvers to perform structured reasoning, to music. Results reveal a clear perceptual gap: models perform near ceiling on MIDI but show accuracy drops on audio. Reasoning and few-shot prompting offer minimal gains. This is expected for MIDI, where performance reaches saturation, but more surprising for audio, where LogicLM, despite near-perfect MIDI accuracy, remains notably brittle. Among models, Gemini Pro achieves the highest performance across most conditions. Overall, current systems reason well over symbols (MIDI) but do not yet \"listen\" reliably from audio. Our method and dataset make the perception-reasoning boundary explicit and offer actionable guidance for building robust, audio-first music systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on AI for Music (AI4Music), 16 pages, 1 figure, 3 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22455","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:47Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:15:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22443","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22443","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22443","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vijay","familyName":"Veerabadran","name":"Veerabadran, Vijay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanyi","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Fanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nitin","familyName":"Kamra","name":"Kamra, Nitin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Matias","name":"Matias, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joy","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Joy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caley","familyName":"Drooff","name":"Drooff, Caley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brett D","familyName":"Roads","name":"Roads, Brett D","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Riley","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, Riley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan","familyName":"Henderson","name":"Henderson, Ethan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuanyi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xuanyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin","familyName":"Carlberg","name":"Carlberg, Kevin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joseph","familyName":"Tighe","name":"Tighe, Joseph","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karl","familyName":"Ridgeway","name":"Ridgeway, Karl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Benchmarking Egocentric Multimodal Goal Inference for Assistive Wearable Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T21:54:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:44:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There has been a surge of interest in assistive wearable agents: agents embodied in wearable form factors (e.g., smart glasses) who take assistive actions toward a user's goal/query (e.g. \"Where did I leave my keys?\"). In this work, we consider the important complementary problem of inferring that goal from multi-modal contextual observations. Solving this \"goal inference\" problem holds the promise of eliminating the effort needed to interact with such an agent. This work focuses on creating WAGIBench, a strong benchmark to measure progress in solving this problem using vision-language models (VLMs). Given the limited prior work in this area, we collected a novel dataset comprising 29 hours of multimodal data from 348 participants across 3,477 recordings, featuring ground-truth goals alongside accompanying visual, audio, digital, and longitudinal contextual observations. We validate that human performance exceeds model performance, achieving 93% multiple-choice accuracy compared with 84% for the best-performing VLM. Generative benchmark results that evaluate several families of modern vision-language models show that larger models perform significantly better on the task, yet remain far from practical usefulness, as they produce relevant goals only 55% of the time. Through a modality ablation, we show that models benefit from extra information in relevant modalities with minimal performance degradation from irrelevant modalities."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted as a spotlight paper at the 39th Conference on Neural Information Processing Systems (NeurIPS 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22443","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:15:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22370","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22370","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22370","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seyed Ahmad Hosseini","familyName":"Miangoleh","name":"Miangoleh, Seyed Ahmad Hosseini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin Jalal","familyName":"Aghdasian","name":"Aghdasian, Amin Jalal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farzaneh","familyName":"Abdollahi","name":"Abdollahi, Farzaneh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"BLIP-FusePPO: A Vision-Language Deep Reinforcement Learning Framework for Lane Keeping in Autonomous Vehicles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T17:27:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose Bootstrapped Language-Image Pretraining-driven Fused State Representation in Proximal Policy Optimization (BLIP-FusePPO), a novel multimodal reinforcement learning (RL) framework for autonomous lane-keeping (LK), in which semantic embeddings generated by a vision-language model (VLM) are directly fused with geometric states, LiDAR observations, and Proportional-Integral-Derivative-based (PID) control feedback within the agent observation space. The proposed method lets the agent learn driving rules that are aware of their surroundings and easy to understand by combining high-level scene understanding from the VLM with low-level control and spatial signals. Our architecture brings together semantic, geometric, and control-aware representations to make policy learning more robust. A hybrid reward function that includes semantic alignment, LK accuracy, obstacle avoidance, and speed regulation helps learning to be more efficient and generalizable. Our method is different from the approaches that only use semantic models to shape rewards. Instead, it directly embeds semantic features into the state representation. This cuts down on expensive runtime inference and makes sure that semantic guidance is always available. The simulation results show that the proposed model is better at LK stability and adaptability than the best vision-based and multimodal RL baselines in a wide range of difficult driving situations. We make our code publicly available."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"https://github.com/Amin-A96/BLIP-FusePPO-A-Vision-Language-Deep-Reinforcement-Learning-Framework-for-Lane-Keeping-in-Autonomous.git"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22370","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:46Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:47Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22366","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22366","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22366","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jindong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jindong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiming","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Weiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nenghai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Nenghai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kejiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"T2SMark: Balancing Robustness and Diversity in Noise-as-Watermark for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T16:55:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:40:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have advanced rapidly in recent years, producing high-fidelity images while raising concerns about intellectual property protection and the misuse of generative AI. Image watermarking for diffusion models, particularly Noise-as-Watermark (NaW) methods, encode watermark as specific standard Gaussian noise vector for image generation, embedding the infomation seamlessly while maintaining image quality. For detection, the generation process is inverted to recover the initial noise vector containing the watermark before extraction. However, existing NaW methods struggle to balance watermark robustness with generation diversity. Some methods achieve strong robustness by heavily constraining initial noise sampling, which degrades user experience, while others preserve diversity but prove too fragile for real-world deployment. To address this issue, we propose T2SMark, a two-stage watermarking scheme based on Tail-Truncated Sampling (TTS). Unlike prior methods that simply map bits to positive or negative values, TTS enhances robustness by embedding bits exclusively in the reliable tail regions while randomly sampling the central zone to preserve the latent distribution. Our two-stage framework then ensures sampling diversity by integrating a randomly generated session key into both encryption pipelines. We evaluate T2SMark on diffusion models with both U-Net and DiT backbones. Extensive experiments show that it achieves an optimal balance between robustness and diversity. Our code is available at \\href{https://github.com/0xD009/T2SMark}{https://github.com/0xD009/T2SMark}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22366","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:13:41Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:13:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:13:41Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22230","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22230","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22230","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziqi","familyName":"Diao","name":"Diao, Ziqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Le","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Le","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shi","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Shi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust MIMO Channel Estimation Using Energy-Based Generative Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T09:26:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:31:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Channel estimation for massive multiple-input multiple-output (MIMO) systems is fundamentally constrained by excessive pilot overhead and high estimation latency. To overcome these obstacles, recent studies have leveraged deep generative networks to capture the prior distribution of wireless channels. In this paper, we propose a novel estimation framework that integrates an energy-based generative diffusion model (DM) with the Metropolis-Hastings (MH) principle. By reparameterizing the diffusion process with an incorporated energy function, the framework explicitly estimates the unnormalized log-prior, while MH corrections refine the sampling trajectory, mitigate deviations, and enhance robustness, ultimately enabling accurate posterior sampling for high-fidelity channel estimation. Numerical results reveal that the proposed approach significantly improves estimation accuracy compared with conventional parameterized DMs and other baseline methods, particularly in cases with limited pilot overhead."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 4 figures, 1 table. This work has been submitted to the IEEE for possible publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22230","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:10:25Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:10:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:10:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22127","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22127","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22127","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxuan","familyName":"Bao","name":"Bao, Wenxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruxi","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Ruxi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingrui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Jingrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mint: A Simple Test-Time Adaptation of Vision-Language Models against Common Corruptions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-25T02:55:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:23:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pretrained vision-language models such as CLIP achieve strong zero-shot generalization but remain vulnerable to distribution shifts caused by input corruptions. In this work, we investigate how corruptions affect CLIP's image embeddings and uncover a consistent phenomenon we term as embedding variance collapse, where both intra-class and inter-class variances shrink as corruption severity increases. We find that this collapse is closely tied to performance degradation, with inter-class variance strongly correlated with classification accuracy. To explain this phenomenon, we analyze how corruptions alter the structure of the embedding space. Our theoretical results suggest that the visual encoder tends to encode corruption-related signals, which dilute class-discriminative features and compress the representation geometry. We further show that maximizing inter-class variance, even when estimated from pseudo-labels, can provably enhance embedding quality. Based on this insight, we propose Mint, a simple test-time adaptation method that maximizes pseudo-label-based inter-class variance on the fly using a mean accumulator and a gradient accumulator. Mint operates effectively with small batch sizes and consistently improves performance across multiple corruption benchmarks and CLIP architectures. Our code is available at https://github.com/baowenxuan/Mint ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22127","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:07:56Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:07:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:07:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22008","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.22008","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.22008","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Saiful Islam","familyName":"Sajol","name":"Sajol, Md Saiful Islam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Magesh","familyName":"Rajasekaran","name":"Rajasekaran, Magesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayden","familyName":"Gemeinhardt","name":"Gemeinhardt, Hayden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Bess","name":"Bess, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Alvin","name":"Alvin, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Supratik","familyName":"Mukhopadhyay","name":"Mukhopadhyay, Supratik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal Human Protein Embeddings Database: DeepDrug Protein Embeddings Bank (DPEB)"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Molecular Networks (q-bio.MN)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Biological sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T20:22:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:12:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Computationally predicting protein-protein interactions (PPIs) is challenging due to the lack of integrated, multimodal protein representations. DPEB is a curated collection of 22,043 human proteins that integrates four embedding types: structural (AlphaFold2), transformer-based sequence (BioEmbeddings), contextual amino acid patterns (ESM-2: Evolutionary Scale Modeling), and sequence-based n-gram statistics (ProtVec]). AlphaFold2 protein structures are available through public databases (e.g., AlphaFold2 Protein Structure Database), but the internal neural network embeddings are not. DPEB addresses this gap by providing AlphaFold2-derived embeddings for computational modeling. Our benchmark evaluations show GraphSAGE with BioEmbedding achieved the highest PPI prediction performance (87.37% AUROC, 79.16% accuracy). The framework also achieved 77.42% accuracy for enzyme classification and 86.04% accuracy for protein family classification. DPEB supports multiple graph neural network methods for PPI prediction, enabling applications in systems biology, drug target identification, pathway analysis, and disease mechanism studies."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.22008","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:05:07Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:05:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:05:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21906","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21906","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21906","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kang Hao","familyName":"Cheong","name":"Cheong, Kang Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Structure-Aware Cooperative Ensemble Evolutionary Optimization on Combinatorial Problems with Multimodal Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Neural and Evolutionary Computing (cs.NE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-24T16:37:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evolutionary algorithms (EAs) have proven effective in exploring the vast solution spaces typical of graph-structured combinatorial problems. However, traditional encoding schemes, such as binary or numerical representations, often fail to straightforwardly capture the intricate structural properties of networks. Through employing the image-based encoding to preserve topological context, this study utilizes multimodal large language models (MLLMs) as evolutionary operators to facilitate structure-aware optimization over graph data. To address the visual clutter inherent in large-scale network visualizations, we leverage graph sparsification techniques to simplify structures while maintaining essential structural features. To further improve robustness and mitigate bias from different sparsification views, we propose a cooperative evolutionary optimization framework that facilitates cross-domain knowledge transfer and unifies multiple sparsified variants of diverse structures. Additionally, recognizing the sensitivity of MLLMs to network layout, we introduce an ensemble strategy that aggregates outputs from various layout configurations through consensus voting. Finally, experiments on real-world networks through various tasks demonstrate that our approach improves both the quality and reliability of solutions in MLLM-driven evolutionary optimization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21906","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:02:44Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:02:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:02:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21881","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21881","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21881","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nannan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Nannan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuanyu","familyName":"Qin","name":"Qin, Chuanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shipeng","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Shipeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Man","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Man","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GeoThought: A Dataset for Enhancing Mathematical Geometry Reasoning in Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T16:43:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have demonstrated strong reasoning capabilities in text-based mathematical problem solving; however, when adapted to visual reasoning tasks, particularly geometric problem solving, their performance substantially declines because geometric problems present unique challenges. Specifically, these challenges stem from two key factors: first, the intrinsic complexity of geometry requiring detailed image comprehension and multi-step reasoning, and second, the limitations of existing datasets which lack sufficient scale, diversity, and explicit reasoning traces, consequently hindering effective model training. To address these challenges, we developed the GeoThoughts dataset, a comprehensive geometric reasoning corpus with two subsets: Geo-Thought-6K with 6,243 samples and its augmented version Geo-Thought-Augmented-10K containing 10,834 samples. Each entry includes visual descriptions, step-by-step solutions, explicit reasoning chains, reflection steps, and final answers. Using this dataset, we developed GeoThought-MLLM, a mathematical reasoning multimodal model that generates detailed thinking processes during problem-solving. Our model outperforms existing benchmarks in geometric tasks, demonstrating that training with our Chain-of-Thought dataset improves geometric reasoning capabilities across both in-domain and out-of-domain settings. Finally, we analyze failure cases and observe that errors primarily arise from incorrect interpretation of mathematical concepts or spatial misjudgment. By invoking CoT to correct these mistakes, the model produces correct answers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21881","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:02:09Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:02:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:02:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21867","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21867","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21867","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haicheng","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Haicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bonan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Bonan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junxian","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Junxian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengyue","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chengyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengbin","familyName":"He","name":"He, Zhengbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guohui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Guohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chengzhong","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chengzhong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenning","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhenning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Addressing Corner Cases in Autonomous Driving: A World Model-based Approach with Mixture of Experts and LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T11:41:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurate and reliable motion forecasting is essential for the safe deployment of autonomous vehicles (AVs), particularly in rare but safety-critical scenarios known as corner cases. Existing models often underperform in these situations due to an over-representation of common scenes in training data and limited generalization capabilities. To address this limitation, we present WM-MoE, the first world model-based motion forecasting framework that unifies perception, temporal memory, and decision making to address the challenges of high-risk corner-case scenarios. The model constructs a compact scene representation that explains current observations, anticipates future dynamics, and evaluates the outcomes of potential actions. To enhance long-horizon reasoning, we leverage large language models (LLMs) and introduce a lightweight temporal tokenizer that maps agent trajectories and contextual cues into the LLM's feature space without additional training, enriching temporal context and commonsense priors. Furthermore, a mixture-of-experts (MoE) is introduced to decompose complex corner cases into subproblems and allocate capacity across scenario types, and a router assigns scenes to specialized experts that infer agent intent and perform counterfactual rollouts. In addition, we introduce nuScenes-corner, a new benchmark that comprises four real-world corner-case scenarios for rigorous evaluation. Extensive experiments on four benchmark datasets (nuScenes, NGSIM, HighD, and MoCAD) showcase that WM-MoE consistently outperforms state-of-the-art (SOTA) baselines and remains robust under corner-case and data-missing conditions, indicating the promise of world model-based architectures for robust and generalizable motion forecasting in fully AVs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21867","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:49Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21866","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21866","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21866","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier","familyName":"Marín","name":"Marín, Javier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Capability Ceilings in Autoregressive Language Models: Empirical Evidence from Knowledge-Intensive Tasks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-23T11:09:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:04:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We document empirical capability ceilings in decoder-only autoregressive language models across knowledge-intensive tasks. Systematic evaluation of OPT and Pythia model families (70M-30B parameters, spanning 240 times scaling) reveals that knowledge retrieval tasks show negligible accuracy improvement despite smooth loss reduction. On MMLU mathematics benchmarks, accuracy remains flat at 19-20% (below 25% random chance) across all scales while cross-entropy loss decreases by 31%. In contrast, procedural tasks like arithmetic show conventional scaling where both metrics improve together. Attention intervention experiments reveal high sensitivity to perturbation: swapping attention patterns between models causes catastrophic performance collapse (complete accuracy loss) rather than graceful degradation. These measurements have immediate engineering implications: for knowledge-intensive applications using OPT and Pythia architectures, parameter scaling beyond 1-2B offers minimal accuracy gains despite continued loss improvement. Our findings quantify capability-specific scaling failures in these model families to inform resource allocation decisions. Whether these patterns reflect fundamental constraints of decoder-only architectures or implementation-specific limitations remains an open question requiring investigation across diverse architectural approaches."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"The experiments in this paper were performed in January 2024. Current model architectures are considerably more complex than those presented here"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21866","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:48Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyi","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Kaiyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Junchao","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Junchao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenlong","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Wenlong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Fei","name":"Fei, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wanli","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Wanli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SynCast: Synergizing Contradictions in Precipitation Nowcasting via Diffusion Sequential Preference Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T16:11:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Precipitation nowcasting based on radar echoes plays a crucial role in monitoring extreme weather and supporting disaster prevention. Although deep learning approaches have achieved significant progress, they still face notable limitations. For example, deterministic models tend to produce over-smoothed predictions, which struggle to capture extreme events and fine-scale precipitation patterns. Probabilistic generative models, due to their inherent randomness, often show fluctuating performance across different metrics and rarely achieve consistently optimal results. Furthermore, precipitation nowcasting is typically evaluated using multiple metrics, some of which are inherently conflicting. For instance, there is often a trade-off between the Critical Success Index (CSI) and the False Alarm Ratio (FAR), making it challenging for existing models to deliver forecasts that perform well on both metrics simultaneously. To address these challenges, we introduce preference optimization into precipitation nowcasting for the first time, motivated by the success of reinforcement learning from human feedback in large language models. Specifically, we propose SynCast, a method that employs the two-stage post-training framework of Diffusion Sequential Preference Optimization (Diffusion-SPO), to progressively align conflicting metrics and consistently achieve superior performance. In the first stage, the framework focuses on reducing FAR, training the model to effectively suppress false alarms. Building on this foundation, the second stage further optimizes CSI with constraints that preserve FAR alignment, thereby achieving synergistic improvements across these conflicting metrics."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:21Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21835","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21835","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21835","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nayan Kumar","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Nayan Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Multimodal, Multitask System for Generating E Commerce Text Listings from Images"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T11:50:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Manually generating catchy descriptions and names is labor intensive and a slow process for retailers. Although generative AI provides an automation solution in form of Vision to Language Models (VLM), the current VLMs are prone to factual \"hallucinations\". Siloed, single task models are not only inefficient but also fail to capture interdependent relationships between features. To address these challenges, we propose an end to end, multi task system that generates factually grounded textual listings from a single image. The contributions of this study are two proposals for the model architecture. First, application of multi task learning approach for fine tuning a vision encoder where a single vision backbone is jointly trained on attribute prediction such as color, hemline and neck style and price regression. Second, introduction of a hierarchical generation process where the model's own predicted attributes are embedded in a prompt and fed to the text decoder to improve factual consistency. The experiments demonstrate the superiority of this architecture. The multi tasking approach outperforms both the independent price regression, with a 3.6% better R2 Value and attribute classification, with a 6.6% improvement F1 score. Critically, the hierarchical generation process proves highly effective, slashing the factual hallucination rate from 12.7% to 7.1%, a 44.5% relative reduction, compared to a non hierarchical ablation. The hierarchical approach also reduces the latency of the autoregressive text generation process by a factor of 3.5 when compared to direct vision to language model of similar size. One minor caveat is that the model does perform 3.5% worse than direct vision-to-language model on ROUGE-L score."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"24 pages, 10 figures, 11 tables. Code can be found at: https://github.com/SinghNayanKumar/multimodal-product-lister/"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21835","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:01:04Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:01:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:01:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi","familyName":"Yin","name":"Yin, Yi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuntao","familyName":"Shou","name":"Shou, Yuntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zao","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Zao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Yun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tao","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Tao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Ai","name":"Ai, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keqin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Keqin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Flow Model with Low-Rank Transformers for Incomplete Multimodal Survival Analysis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-22T02:27:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:03:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In recent years, multimodal medical data-based survival analysis has attracted much attention. However, real-world datasets often suffer from the problem of incomplete modality, where some patient modality information is missing due to acquisition limitations or system failures. Existing methods typically infer missing modalities directly from observed ones using deep neural networks, but they often ignore the distributional discrepancy across modalities, resulting in inconsistent and unreliable modality reconstruction. To address these challenges, we propose a novel framework that combines a low-rank Transformer with a flow-based generative model for robust and flexible multimodal survival prediction. Specifically, we first formulate the concerned problem as incomplete multimodal survival analysis using the multi-instance representation of whole slide images (WSIs) and genomic profiles. To realize incomplete multimodal survival analysis, we propose a class-specific flow for cross-modal distribution alignment. Under the condition of class labels, we model and transform the cross-modal distribution. By virtue of the reversible structure and accurate density modeling capabilities of the normalizing flow model, the model can effectively construct a distribution-consistent latent space of the missing modality, thereby improving the consistency between the reconstructed data and the true distribution. Finally, we design a lightweight Transformer architecture to model intra-modal dependencies while alleviating the overfitting problem in high-dimensional modality fusion by virtue of the low-rank Transformer. Extensive experiments have demonstrated that our method not only achieves state-of-the-art performance under complete modality settings, but also maintains robust and superior accuracy under the incomplete modalities scenario."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:56Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21812","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21812","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21812","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chanyoung","familyName":"Chung","name":"Chung, Chanyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyeongryul","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Kyeongryul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sunbin","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sunbin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joyce Jiyoung","familyName":"Whang","name":"Whang, Joyce Jiyoung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unifying Inductive, Cross-Domain, and Multimodal Learning for Robust and Generalizable Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-21T10:26:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recommender systems have long been built upon the modeling of interactions between users and items, while recent studies have sought to broaden this paradigm by generalizing to new users and items, incorporating diverse information sources, and transferring knowledge across domains. Nevertheless, these efforts have largely focused on individual aspects, hindering their ability to tackle the complex recommendation scenarios that arise in daily consumptions across diverse domains. In this paper, we present MICRec, a unified framework that fuses inductive modeling, multimodal guidance, and cross-domain transfer to capture user contexts and latent preferences in heterogeneous and incomplete real-world data. Moving beyond the inductive backbone of INMO, our model refines expressive representations through modality-based aggregation and alleviates data sparsity by leveraging overlapping users as anchors across domains, thereby enabling robust and generalizable recommendation. Experiments show that MICRec outperforms 12 baselines, with notable gains in domains with limited training data."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 3 figures, and 4 tables. International Workshop on Multimodal Generative Search and Recommendation (MMGenSR) at The 34th ACM International Conference on Information and Knowledge Management (CIKM 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21812","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:32Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21794","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21794","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21794","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kejia","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Kejia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiawen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacong","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Jiacong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kewei","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Kewei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zunlei","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Zunlei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingli","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Mingli","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Token-Level Inference-Time Alignment for Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-20T09:58:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have become essential backbones of modern multimodal intelligence, yet their outputs remain prone to hallucination-plausible text misaligned with visual inputs. Existing alignment approaches often rely on expensive fine-tuning with annotated preference data or sequence-level inference strategies that provide only coarse, delayed feedback. To overcome these limitations, we present TITA (Token-level Inference-Time Alignment), a lightweight framework that freezes the base VLM and instead trains a reward model to approximate its distribution. During inference, implicit preference signals are extracted as log-probability ratios between the reward model and the target VLM, yielding dense autoregressive feedback. This formulation can be viewed as an inference-time variant of Direct Preference Optimization (DPO), providing token-level corrective signals without retraining the backbone. Extensive evaluations on LLaVA-1.5-7B and 13B show consistent gains across 12 benchmarks, with improvements of 8.6% on MMVet and 6.7% on POPE, indicating stronger general understanding and reduced hallucinations. Additional experiments on Qwen2.5-VL-7B and DeepSeek-VL2-27.5B show comparable gains, especially in hallucination reduction and VQA accuracy, while incurring negligible inference overhead."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21794","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:05Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21792","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21792","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21792","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shifeng","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Shifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanzhu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yanzhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adams Wai-Kin","familyName":"Kong","name":"Kong, Adams Wai-Kin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Variance-Reduction Guidance: Sampling Trajectory Optimization for Diffusion Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-20T03:24:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion models have become emerging generative models. Their sampling process involves multiple steps, and in each step the models predict the noise from a noisy sample. When the models make prediction, the output deviates from the ground truth, and we call such a deviation as \\textit{prediction error}. The prediction error accumulates over the sampling process and deteriorates generation quality. This paper introduces a novel technique for statistically measuring the prediction error and proposes the Variance-Reduction Guidance (VRG) method to mitigate this error. VRG does not require model fine-tuning or modification. Given a predefined sampling trajectory, it searches for a new trajectory which has the same number of sampling steps but produces higher quality results. VRG is applicable to both conditional and unconditional generation. Experiments on various datasets and baselines demonstrate that VRG can significantly improve the generation quality of diffusion models. Source code is available at https://github.com/shifengxu/VRG."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21792","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:02Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21791","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21791","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21791","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niraj","familyName":"Chaudhari","name":"Chaudhari, Niraj","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manmeet","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Manmeet","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naveen","familyName":"Sudharsan","name":"Sudharsan, Naveen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amit Kumar","familyName":"Srivastava","name":"Srivastava, Amit Kumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Kamath","name":"Kamath, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dushyant","familyName":"Mahajan","name":"Mahajan, Dushyant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayan","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Ayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the design space of diffusion and flow models for data fusion"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-20T01:03:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data fusion is an essential task in various domains, enabling the integration of multi-source information to enhance data quality and insights. One key application is in satellite remote sensing, where fusing multi-sensor observations can improve spatial and temporal resolution. In this study, we explore the design space of diffusion and flow models for data fusion, focusing on the integration of Defense Meteorological Satellite Program's Operational Linescan System (DMSP-OLS) and Visible Infrared Imaging Radiometer Suite (VIIRS) nighttime lights data. Our approach leverages a diverse set of 2D image-to-image generative models, including UNET, diffusion, and flow modeling architectures. We evaluate the effectiveness of these architectures in satellite remote sensing data fusion, identifying diffusion models based on UNet as particularly adept at preserving fine-grained spatial details and generating high-fidelity fused images. We also provide guidance on the selection of noise schedulers in diffusion-based models, highlighting the trade-offs between iterative solvers for faster inference and discrete schedulers for higher-quality reconstructions. Additionally, we explore quantization techniques to optimize memory efficiency and computational cost without compromising performance. Our findings offer practical insights into selecting the most effective diffusion and flow model architectures for data fusion tasks, particularly in remote sensing applications, and provide recommendations for leveraging noise scheduling strategies to enhance fusion quality."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21791","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T03:00:00Z","registered":"2025-10-28T03:00:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T03:00:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21761","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21761","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21761","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesse","familyName":"Atuhurra","name":"Atuhurra, Jesse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hidetaka","familyName":"Kamigaito","name":"Kamigaito, Hidetaka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Taro","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Taro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Koichiro","familyName":"Yoshino","name":"Yoshino, Koichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"J-ORA: A Framework and Multimodal Dataset for Japanese Object Identification, Reference, Action Prediction in Robot Perception"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-13T04:53:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:01:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce J-ORA, a novel multimodal dataset that bridges the gap in robot perception by providing detailed object attribute annotations within Japanese human-robot dialogue scenarios. J-ORA is designed to support three critical perception tasks, object identification, reference resolution, and next-action prediction, by leveraging a comprehensive template of attributes (e.g., category, color, shape, size, material, and spatial relations). Extensive evaluations with both proprietary and open-source Vision Language Models (VLMs) reveal that incorporating detailed object attributes substantially improves multimodal perception performance compared to without object attributes. Despite the improvement, we find that there still exists a gap between proprietary and open-source VLMs. In addition, our analysis of object affordances demonstrates varying abilities in understanding object functionality and contextual relationships across different VLMs. These findings underscore the importance of rich, context-sensitive attribute annotations in advancing robot perception in dynamic environments. See project page at https://jatuhurrra.github.io/J-ORA/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to IROS2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21761","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T02:59:19Z","registered":"2025-10-28T02:59:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T02:59:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21757","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21757","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21757","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mihir","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Mihir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pratik","familyName":"Desai","name":"Desai, Pratik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ross","familyName":"Greer","name":"Greer, Ross","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agro-Consensus: Semantic Self-Consistency in Vision-Language Models for Crop Disease Management in Developing Countries"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-11T19:41:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Agricultural disease management in developing countries such as India, Kenya, and Nigeria faces significant challenges due to limited access to expert plant pathologists, unreliable internet connectivity, and cost constraints that hinder the deployment of large-scale AI systems. This work introduces a cost-effective self-consistency framework to improve vision-language model (VLM) reliability for agricultural image captioning. The proposed method employs semantic clustering, using a lightweight (80MB) pre-trained embedding model to group multiple candidate responses. It then selects the most coherent caption -- containing a diagnosis, symptoms, analysis, treatment, and prevention recommendations -- through a cosine similarity-based consensus. A practical human-in-the-loop (HITL) component is incorporated, wherein user confirmation of the crop type filters erroneous generations, ensuring higher-quality input for the consensus mechanism. Applied to the publicly available PlantVillage dataset using a fine-tuned 3B-parameter PaliGemma model, our framework demonstrates improvements over standard decoding methods. Evaluated on 800 crop disease images with up to 21 generations per image, our single-cluster consensus method achieves a peak accuracy of 83.1% with 10 candidate generations, compared to the 77.5% baseline accuracy of greedy decoding. The framework's effectiveness is further demonstrated when considering multiple clusters; accuracy rises to 94.0% when a correct response is found within any of the top four candidate clusters, outperforming the 88.5% achieved by a top-4 selection from the baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21757","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T02:59:13Z","registered":"2025-10-28T02:59:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T02:59:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21740","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21740","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21740","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexa R.","familyName":"Tartaglini","name":"Tartaglini, Alexa R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satchel","familyName":"Grant","name":"Grant, Satchel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Wurgaft","name":"Wurgaft, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christopher","familyName":"Potts","name":"Potts, Christopher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Judith E.","familyName":"Fan","name":"Fan, Judith E.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Diagnosing Bottlenecks in Data Visualization Understanding by Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-02T18:29:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:00:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Data visualizations are vital components of many scientific articles and news stories. Current vision-language models (VLMs) still struggle on basic data visualization understanding tasks, but the causes of failure remain unclear. Are VLM failures attributable to limitations in how visual information in the data visualization is encoded, how information is transferred between the vision and language modules, or how information is processed within the language module? We developed FUGU, a suite of data visualization understanding tasks, to precisely characterize potential sources of difficulty (e.g., extracting the position of data points, distances between them, and other summary statistics). We used FUGU to investigate three widely used VLMs. To diagnose the sources of errors produced by these models, we used activation patching and linear probes to trace information flow through models across a variety of prompting strategies. We found that some models fail to generate the coordinates of individual data points correctly, and these initial errors often lead to erroneous final responses. When these models are provided with the correct coordinates, performance improves substantially. Moreover, even when the model generates an incorrect response, the correct coordinates can be successfully read out from the latent representations in the vision encoder, suggesting that the source of these errors lies in the vision-language handoff. We further found that while providing correct coordinates helps with tasks involving one or a small number of data points, it generally worsens performance for tasks that require extracting statistical relationships across many data points. Fine-tuning models on FUGU also fails to yield ceiling performance. These findings point to architectural constraints in current VLMs that might pose significant challenges for reliable data visualization understanding."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21740","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T02:58:49Z","registered":"2025-10-28T02:58:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T02:58:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21722","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.21722","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.21722","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beitong","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Beitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingzhi","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Lingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haozhen","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Haozhen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingcheng","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jingcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyuan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Mingyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Deepak","familyName":"Vasisht","name":"Vasisht, Deepak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Klara","familyName":"Nahrstedt","name":"Nahrstedt, Klara","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AquaVLM: Improving Underwater Situation Awareness with Mobile Vision Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-17T04:16:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-28T00:00:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Underwater activities like scuba diving enable millions annually to explore marine environments for recreation and scientific research. Maintaining situational awareness and effective communication are essential for diver safety. Traditional underwater communication systems are often bulky and expensive, limiting their accessibility to divers of all levels. While recent systems leverage lightweight smartphones and support text messaging, the messages are predefined and thus restrict context-specific communication.\n In this paper, we present AquaVLM, a tap-and-send underwater communication system that automatically generates context-aware messages and transmits them using ubiquitous smartphones. Our system features a mobile vision-language model (VLM) fine-tuned on an auto-generated underwater conversation dataset and employs a hierarchical message generation pipeline. We co-design the VLM and transmission, incorporating error-resilient fine-tuning to improve the system's robustness to transmission errors. We develop a VR simulator to enable users to experience AquaVLM in a realistic underwater environment and create a fully functional prototype on the iOS platform for real-world experiments. Both subjective and objective evaluations validate the effectiveness of AquaVLM and highlight its potential for personal underwater communication as well as broader mobile VLM applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 10 figures, under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.21722","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-28T02:58:24Z","registered":"2025-10-28T02:58:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-28T02:58:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":73,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-27T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-28T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28titles.title%3A%28multimodal+OR+%22multi-modal%22+OR+%22vision+language%22+OR+%22vision-language%22+OR+%22world+model%22+OR+%22world+models%22+OR+VLA+OR+diffusion+OR+autoregressive%29+AND+descriptions.description%3A%28%22foundation+model%22+OR+%22foundation+models%22+OR+%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+generative+OR+transformer%29%29\u0026client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1"}}